window.WB_BOOK=window.WB_BOOK||{};window.WB_BOOK["week07"]={"id":"week07","n":7,"section":"C","label":"","title":"Waves, Sound & the EM Spectrum","titleText":"Waves, Sound & the EM Spectrum","subtitle":"How energy travels without matter moving — from ripples and music to radio, X-rays and gamma rays","intro":"This week starts Section C. Everything here is built on ONE idea: a wave carries energy from one place to another, but the material it travels through does not travel with it. You will learn the two kinds of wave (transverse and longitudinal), the words used to describe a wave (amplitude, wavelength, frequency, period), the most important wave equation in the course ⟪(v=f\\lambda )⟫, how to read the two kinds of wave graph, everything CSEC asks about sound (including echoes, thunder and ultrasound), and the electromagnetic spectrum. Wave calculations and the EM spectrum appear on almost every Paper 01, and sound/echo questions are very common on Paper 02.","objectives":[{"code":"C1.1","text":"Describe pulses and progressive (travelling) waves; distinguish between transverse and longitudinal waves"},{"code":"C1.2","text":"Use the terms amplitude, wavelength, frequency, period and wave speed; use ⟪v=f\\lambda⟫ and ⟪T=1/f⟫"},{"code":"C1.3","text":"Draw and interpret displacement–position and displacement–time graphs of a wave"},{"code":"C2.1","text":"Explain how sound is produced by vibrating sources and why sound needs a material medium to travel"},{"code":"C2.2","text":"Relate pitch to frequency and loudness to amplitude"},{"code":"C2.3","text":"Determine the speed of sound in air and use it in practical situations (echoes, thunder and lightning)"},{"code":"C2.4","text":"Describe evidence that sound can be reflected, refracted, diffracted and can show interference"},{"code":"C2.5","text":"Describe uses of ultrasound"},{"code":"C3.1","text":"State the properties of electromagnetic waves"},{"code":"C3.2","text":"List the members of the electromagnetic spectrum in order of wavelength (or frequency)"},{"code":"C3.3","text":"Give a source and a use of each type of electromagnetic wave"}],"formulaCard":[{"tex":"v=f\\lambda","meaning":"Wave equation (all waves)","units":"v in m s^−1^, f in Hz, λ in m"},{"tex":"T=1/f","meaning":"Period and frequency","units":"T in s, f in Hz"},{"tex":"f=\\text{ number of waves }\\div \\text{ time}","meaning":"Frequency by counting","units":"Hz"},{"tex":"v=2d/t","meaning":"Echo (sound goes there and back)","units":"m s^−1^"},{"tex":"d=vt","meaning":"Thunder and lightning (one way)","units":"m"},{"tex":"d=vt/2","meaning":"Sonar / echo sounding depth","units":"m"},{"tex":"c=3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}","meaning":"Speed of ALL EM waves in a vacuum","units":"m s^−1^"},{"tex":"v_{\\text{sound}}\\approx 330\\text{–}340\\ (\\text{air}),\\ 1500\\ (\\text{water}),\\ 5000+(\\text{steel})","meaning":"Speed of sound in different media","units":"m s^−1^"},{"text":"20 Hz – 20 kHz","meaning":"Human audible range; ultrasound > 20 kHz","units":"Hz"},{"text":"R  M  I  V  U  X  G","meaning":"EM order: wavelength decreases, frequency & energy increase →","units":"—"}],"glossary":[{"term":"Wave","def":"A disturbance that transfers energy without transferring matter."},{"term":"Medium","def":"The material a wave travels through."},{"term":"Pulse","def":"A single disturbance travelling through a medium."},{"term":"Progressive wave","def":"A continuous travelling wave from a vibrating source."},{"term":"Transverse wave","def":"Particles vibrate at 90° to the direction of travel."},{"term":"Longitudinal wave","def":"Particles vibrate parallel to the direction of travel."},{"term":"Compression","def":"Region of a longitudinal wave where particles are close together."},{"term":"Rarefaction","def":"Region of a longitudinal wave where particles are spread out."},{"term":"Amplitude","def":"Maximum displacement from the rest position."},{"term":"Wavelength","def":"Distance between successive points in phase (crest to crest)."},{"term":"Frequency","def":"Number of complete waves per second (Hz)."},{"term":"Period","def":"Time for one complete wave (s)."},{"term":"Pitch","def":"How high or low a note sounds; depends on frequency."},{"term":"Loudness","def":"How loud a sound is; depends on amplitude."},{"term":"Echo","def":"A reflected sound."},{"term":"Diffraction","def":"Spreading of waves through a gap or round an edge."},{"term":"Interference","def":"Two overlapping waves adding (loud/bright) or cancelling (quiet/dark)."},{"term":"Ultrasound","def":"Sound with frequency above 20 kHz."},{"term":"CRO","def":"Cathode ray oscilloscope — displays a wave as a trace on a screen."},{"term":"Electromagnetic spectrum","def":"The family of EM waves: radio, microwaves, IR, visible, UV, X-rays, gamma."}],"lessons":[{"id":"week07.7.1","kind":"unit","unit":"7.1","title":"What is a Wave? Pulses, Progressive Waves, Transverse and Longitudinal","titleText":"What is a Wave? Pulses, Progressive Waves, Transverse and Longitudinal","goals":[{"code":"C1.1","text":"Describe pulses and progressive (travelling) waves; distinguish between transverse and longitudinal waves"}],"codes":["C1.1"],"words":[{"term":"Wave","def":"A disturbance that transfers energy without transferring matter."},{"term":"Medium","def":"The material a wave travels through."},{"term":"Pulse","def":"A single disturbance travelling through a medium."},{"term":"Progressive wave","def":"A continuous travelling wave from a vibrating source."},{"term":"Transverse wave","def":"Particles vibrate at 90° to the direction of travel."},{"term":"Longitudinal wave","def":"Particles vibrate parallel to the direction of travel."},{"term":"Compression","def":"Region of a longitudinal wave where particles are close together."},{"term":"Rarefaction","def":"Region of a longitudinal wave where particles are spread out."},{"term":"Ultrasound","def":"Sound with frequency above 20 kHz."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"p","text":"Drop a stone into a pond. Ripples spread out to the edge, but the water itself does not rush to the edge. A cork floating on the pond just bobs **up and down** in the same place. What travels outward is **energy**, not water. That is what a wave is."},{"type":"def","term":"Wave","text":"a disturbance that transfers **energy** from one place to another **without** transferring matter (the material of the medium)."},{"type":"img","src":"img/week07/cork","alt":"Figure 7.1 — A cork on a water wave only bobs up and down. The energy moves on; the water does not.","caption":"Figure 7.1 — A cork on a water wave only bobs up and down. The energy moves on; the water does not.","width":5.6,"w":1174,"h":323},{"type":"def","term":"Medium","text":"the material (solid, liquid or gas) that a wave travels through, e.g. air for sound, water for ripples."},{"type":"h","text":"Pulses and progressive waves"},{"type":"def","term":"Pulse","text":"a single disturbance (one 'hump') that travels through a medium — e.g. ONE flick of the end of a rope."},{"type":"def","term":"Progressive (travelling / continuous) wave","text":"a continuous series of disturbances that travels outward from a source which keeps vibrating, transferring energy as it goes."},{"type":"img","src":"img/week07/pulse_wave","alt":"Figure 7.2 — (a) One flick makes a pulse. (b) Shaking continuously makes a progressive wave.","caption":"Figure 7.2 — (a) One flick makes a pulse. (b) Shaking continuously makes a progressive wave.","width":5.8,"w":1163,"h":364},{"type":"tip","text":"Every wave starts with a **vibration** (an oscillation). The source of the wave vibrates; that vibration is passed from particle to particle through the medium."},{"type":"h","text":"The two types of wave"},{"type":"def","term":"Transverse wave","text":"a wave in which the particles of the medium vibrate **at right angles (perpendicular, 90°)** to the direction in which the wave travels."},{"type":"def","term":"Longitudinal wave","text":"a wave in which the particles of the medium vibrate **parallel to (along)** the direction in which the wave travels."},{"type":"img","src":"img/week07/trans_long","alt":"Figure 7.3 — Top: transverse wave on a rope. Bottom: longitudinal wave on a slinky spring showing compressions (C) and rarefactions (R).","caption":"Figure 7.3 — Top: transverse wave on a rope. Bottom: longitudinal wave on a slinky spring showing compressions (C) and rarefactions (R).","width":6,"w":1278,"h":610},{"type":"def","term":"Compression","text":"a region in a longitudinal wave where the particles are pushed **close together** (high pressure)."},{"type":"def","term":"Rarefaction","text":"a region in a longitudinal wave where the particles are **spread out** (low pressure)."},{"type":"table","headers":["","Transverse","Longitudinal"],"rows":[["Vibration of particles","At 90° to direction of travel","Along (parallel to) direction of travel"],["Features you see","Crests (peaks) and troughs","Compressions and rarefactions"],["Slinky demo","Shake the end side-to-side","Push and pull the end back and forth"],["Examples","Light and ALL electromagnetic waves, waves on a string/rope, water ripples (treated as transverse at CSEC)","Sound, ultrasound, the push–pull wave on a slinky"]]},{"type":"h","text":"Demonstration with a slinky spring"},{"type":"steps","items":["Stretch a slinky along a smooth bench or floor with a partner holding the other end.","Tie a small piece of coloured string (a marker) to one coil near the middle.","**Transverse:** move your end quickly side-to-side. The wave travels along; the marker moves side-to-side only.","**Longitudinal:** push and pull your end back and forth along the length of the spring. Compressions and rarefactions travel along; the marker moves back and forth only.","**Conclusion:** in both cases the marker returns to where it started — the wave carries energy, not the coils."]},{"type":"warn","text":"Do not write 'the particles travel with the wave'. The particles only **vibrate about a fixed position**. Only the energy (the disturbance) travels.","label":"Common mistake"},{"type":"remember","text":"Sound = LONGITUDINAL. Light (and every EM wave) = TRANSVERSE. This is tested on almost every Paper 01."}],"examples":[{"id":"week07.7.1.e1","title":"Classifying waves","question":"State whether each wave is transverse or longitudinal: (a) sound in air, (b) light, (c) a wave on a guitar string, (d) ultrasound in the body, (e) radio waves.","steps":[{"label":"Rule","text":"Sound-type waves (sound, ultrasound) push and pull the particles along the direction of travel → longitudinal. EM waves and waves on strings vibrate at 90° → transverse."},{"label":"Apply","text":"(a) longitudinal  (b) transverse  (c) transverse  (d) longitudinal  (e) transverse"}],"answer":"(a) L  (b) T  (c) T  (d) L  (e) T"},{"id":"week07.7.1.e2","title":"Energy, not matter","question":"A surfer waits beyond the breaking waves. When a small wave passes she moves up and then down, but she does not move towards the beach. Explain why.","steps":[{"label":"Idea","text":"A wave transfers energy, not matter."},{"label":"Explain","text":"The water particles (and the surfer floating on them) only vibrate up and down about a fixed position as the wave passes. The energy of the wave moves towards the beach, but the water does not."}],"answer":"The wave transfers energy; the water particles only oscillate up and down about their rest positions, so she is not carried along."}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week07.7.1.p1","kind":"self","q":"Define a **wave**.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"A disturbance that transfers energy (1) from one place to another without transferring matter / the medium (1).","points":[{"text":"A disturbance that transfers energy","marks":1},{"text":"from one place to another without transferring matter / the medium","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Classifying waves). Start like this: Rule: Sound-type waves (sound, ultrasound) push and pull the particles along the direction of travel → longitudinal. EM waves and waves on strings vibrate at 90° → transverse.","from":1}},{"id":"week07.7.1.p2","kind":"self","q":"What is the difference between a **pulse** and a **progressive wave**?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Pulse: a single disturbance (1). Progressive wave: a continuous series of disturbances from a source that keeps vibrating (1).","points":[{"text":"Pulse: a single disturbance","marks":1},{"text":"Progressive wave: a continuous series of disturbances from a source that keeps vibrating","marks":1}],"section":"try"}],"practice":[{"id":"week07.7.1.p3","kind":"self","q":"Define a **transverse** wave and give ONE example.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Particles vibrate at right angles to the direction of travel (1); e.g. light / any EM wave / wave on a rope or string / water ripple (1).","points":[{"text":"Particles vibrate at right angles to the direction of travel","marks":1},{"text":"e.g. light / any EM wave / wave on a rope or string / water ripple","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Classifying waves). Start like this: Rule: Sound-type waves (sound, ultrasound) push and pull the particles along the direction of travel → longitudinal. EM waves and waves on strings vibrate at 90° → transverse.","from":1}},{"id":"week07.7.1.p4","kind":"self","q":"Define a **longitudinal** wave and give ONE example.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Particles vibrate parallel to (along) the direction of travel (1); e.g. sound / ultrasound / push–pull wave on a slinky (1).","points":[{"text":"Particles vibrate parallel to (along) the direction of travel","marks":1},{"text":"e.g. sound / ultrasound / push–pull wave on a slinky","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Classifying waves). Start like this: Rule: Sound-type waves (sound, ultrasound) push and pull the particles along the direction of travel → longitudinal. EM waves and waves on strings vibrate at 90° → transverse.","from":1}},{"id":"week07.7.1.p5","kind":"self","q":"Explain the terms **compression** and **rarefaction**.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Compression: region where particles are close together / high pressure (1). Rarefaction: region where particles are spread out / low pressure (1).","points":[{"text":"Compression: region where particles are close together / high pressure","marks":1},{"text":"Rarefaction: region where particles are spread out / low pressure","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Energy, not matter). Start like this: Idea: A wave transfers energy, not matter.","from":2}},{"id":"week07.7.1.p6","kind":"self","q":"A small piece of tape is stuck to one coil of a slinky. Describe the motion of the tape when (a) a transverse wave and (b) a longitudinal wave passes along the slinky.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"(a) Moves side to side (at 90° to the spring) and returns to its starting position (1). (b) Moves back and forth along the length of the spring about its starting position (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) Moves side to side (at 90° to the spring) and returns to its starting position","marks":1},{"text":"(b) Moves back and forth along the length of the spring about its starting position","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Energy, not matter). Start like this: Idea: A wave transfers energy, not matter.","from":2}}],"exit":[{"id":"week07.7.1.p7","kind":"self","q":"Leaves float on a pond. Ripples pass under them. Describe and explain what happens to the leaves.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Leaves bob up and down (1) and stay in roughly the same place (1) because the wave transfers energy, not water / the water particles only oscillate (1).","points":[{"text":"Leaves bob up and down","marks":1},{"text":"and stay in roughly the same place","marks":1},{"text":"because the wave transfers energy, not water / the water particles only oscillate","marks":1}],"section":"exit"},{"id":"week07.7.1.p8","kind":"self","q":"Copy and complete the table: for SOUND and for LIGHT state (i) transverse or longitudinal, (ii) whether it can travel through a vacuum.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":4,"scheme":"Sound: longitudinal (1), cannot travel through a vacuum (1). Light: transverse (1), can travel through a vacuum (1).","points":[{"text":"Sound: longitudinal","marks":1},{"text":"cannot travel through a vacuum","marks":1},{"text":"Light: transverse","marks":1},{"text":"can travel through a vacuum","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week07.7.1.w1","kind":"num","q":"Convert 57 °C to kelvin.","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"330 K.","points":[{"text":"330 K.","marks":1}],"num":{"value":330,"sf":2,"unit":"K","unitTex":"\\mathrm{K}"},"section":"warmup","src":"week06.r1"},{"id":"week07.7.1.w2","kind":"self","q":"State Boyle's law.","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"Pressure of a fixed mass of gas is inversely proportional to its volume at constant temperature.","points":[{"text":"Pressure of a fixed mass of gas is inversely proportional to its volume at constant temperature.","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week06.r2"},{"id":"week07.7.1.w3","kind":"num","q":"A gas at 300 K and 100 kPa is heated to 600 K at constant volume. New pressure?","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"200 kPa.","points":[{"text":"200 kPa.","marks":1}],"num":{"value":200,"sf":1,"unit":"kPa","unitTex":"\\mathrm{kPa}"},"section":"warmup","src":"week06.r3"},{"id":"week07.7.1.w4","kind":"short","q":"Which method of heat transfer can happen in a vacuum?","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"Radiation.","points":[{"text":"Radiation.","marks":1}],"accept":["Radiation"],"section":"warmup","src":"week06.r4"},{"id":"week07.7.1.w5","kind":"self","q":"Which feature of a vacuum flask reduces radiation?","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"The silvered (shiny) walls.","points":[{"text":"The silvered (shiny) walls.","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week06.r5"}],"quickStart":[{"type":"p","text":"You need three tools from earlier weeks. Check them before Unit 7.1."},{"type":"h","text":"1. Prefixes — convert BEFORE you substitute"},{"type":"table","headers":["Prefix","Symbol","Multiply by","Example"],"rows":[["giga","G","10^9^","⟪2.4\\,\\mathrm{GHz}=2.4\\times 10^{9}\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫"],["mega","M","10^6^","⟪100\\,\\mathrm{MHz}=1.00\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫"],["kilo","k","10^3^","⟪20\\,\\mathrm{kHz}=20\\,000\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫"],["centi","c","10^−2^","⟪40\\,\\mathrm{cm}=0.40\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫"],["milli","m","10^−3^","⟪8\\,\\mathrm{ms}=0.008\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫"],["micro","μ","10^−6^","⟪5\\,\\mathrm{\\mu s}=5\\times 10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫"],["nano","n","10^−9^","⟪600\\,\\mathrm{nm}=6.00\\times 10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫"]]},{"type":"h","text":"2. Speed = distance ÷ time"},{"type":"formula","tex":"v=d/t","where":["⟪v⟫ = speed (m s^−1^), d = distance (m), t = time (s)","Rearranged: ⟪d=v\\times t⟫   and   ⟪t=d/v⟫"]},{"type":"h","text":"3. Standard form on your calculator"},{"type":"bullets","items":["⟪3.0\\times 10^{8}⟫ is typed as **3 EXP 8** (or ×10^x^ 8). Do NOT type ⟪3\\times 10EXP8⟫ — that gives ⟪3\\times 10^{9}⟫.","Example: ⟪3.0\\times 10^{8}\\div 1.00\\times 10^{8}=3.0⟫. Check this on your calculator now."]},{"type":"fill","text":"Quick check: convert 2.5 kHz to Hz, 15 cm to m and 4 ms to s.","lines":2}],"n":47},{"id":"week07.7.2","kind":"unit","unit":"7.2","title":"Describing a Wave: Amplitude, Wavelength, Frequency, Period and ⟪v=f\\lambda⟫","titleText":"Describing a Wave: Amplitude, Wavelength, Frequency, Period and v = fλ","goals":[{"code":"C1.2","text":"Use the terms amplitude, wavelength, frequency, period and wave speed; use ⟪v=f\\lambda⟫ and ⟪T=1/f⟫"}],"codes":["C1.2"],"words":[{"term":"Wave","def":"A disturbance that transfers energy without transferring matter."},{"term":"Medium","def":"The material a wave travels through."},{"term":"Compression","def":"Region of a longitudinal wave where particles are close together."},{"term":"Amplitude","def":"Maximum displacement from the rest position."},{"term":"Wavelength","def":"Distance between successive points in phase (crest to crest)."},{"term":"Frequency","def":"Number of complete waves per second (Hz)."},{"term":"Period","def":"Time for one complete wave (s)."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"p","text":"To describe any wave we use five quantities. Learn the definitions word-for-word — they are free marks."},{"type":"def","term":"Amplitude (a)","text":"the **maximum displacement** of a particle from its rest (undisturbed) position. Unit: metre (m). It is measured from the middle line to a crest — NOT from crest to trough."},{"type":"def","term":"Wavelength (λ)","text":"the distance between two **successive** particles that are **in phase**, e.g. the distance from one crest to the next crest (or one compression to the next compression). Unit: m."},{"type":"def","term":"Frequency (f)","text":"the **number of complete waves** (oscillations) produced (or passing a point) **per second**. Unit: hertz (Hz); ⟪1\\,\\mathrm{Hz}=1⟫ wave per second."},{"type":"def","term":"Period (T)","text":"the **time taken for one complete** wave (oscillation) to pass a point. Unit: second (s)."},{"type":"def","term":"Wave speed (v)","text":"the distance travelled by the wave (crest) per second. Unit: m s^−1^."},{"type":"img","src":"img/week07/wave_dp","alt":"Figure 7.4 — Amplitude and wavelength shown on a wave.","caption":"Figure 7.4 — Amplitude and wavelength shown on a wave.","width":5.4,"w":1214,"h":654},{"type":"def","term":"In phase","text":"two points on a wave are in phase if they are moving in the **same direction** with the **same displacement** at the same time (e.g. two crests)."},{"type":"h","text":"The two formulas you must know"},{"type":"formula","tex":"T=1/f\\qquad (\\text{and }f=1/T)","where":["⟪T⟫ = period (s)","⟪f⟫ = frequency (Hz)","Example: ⟪f=50\\,\\mathrm{Hz}\\rightarrow T=1/50=0.02\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫"]},{"type":"formula","tex":"v=f\\lambda","where":["⟪v⟫ = wave speed (m s^−1^)","⟪f⟫ = frequency (Hz)","⟪\\lambda⟫ = wavelength (m)","Rearranged: ⟪f=v/\\lambda⟫   and   ⟪\\lambda =v/f⟫"],"label":"The wave equation"},{"type":"p","text":"**Why **⟪\\boldsymbol{v=f\\lambda }⟫** works:** in one second, f waves leave the source. Each wave is λ long. So the front of the wave has travelled ⟪f\\times \\lambda⟫ metres in one second — that is its speed."},{"type":"tip","text":"Use the triangle: cover the one you want. v on top; f and λ on the bottom. Always convert to Hz and m first (kHz → Hz, MHz → Hz, cm → m)."},{"type":"table","headers":["Quantity","Symbol","SI unit","What it depends on"],"rows":[["Amplitude","a","m","Energy given by the source (bigger amplitude = more energy)"],["Wavelength","λ","m","Source frequency AND the medium"],["Frequency","f","Hz","The SOURCE only — it does not change when a wave enters a new medium"],["Period","T","s","The source ⟪(T=1/f)⟫"],["Speed","v","m s^−1^","The MEDIUM (e.g. sound is faster in water than in air)"]]},{"type":"remember","text":"When a wave passes into a different medium its **frequency stays the same**; its speed changes, so its wavelength changes too ⟪(\\lambda =v/f)⟫."},{"type":"h","text":"Method for every wave calculation"},{"type":"steps","items":["Write the formula (⟪v=f\\lambda⟫ or ⟪T=1/f⟫).","List the values and CONVERT units (kHz → Hz, cm → m, ms → s).","Substitute.","Calculate and give the answer with its unit and sensible significant figures."]},{"type":"warn","text":"Amplitude is NOT crest-to-trough. If the crest-to-trough height is 8 cm, the amplitude is 4 cm.","label":"Common mistake"}],"examples":[{"id":"week07.7.2.e1","title":"Speed of a sound wave","question":"A tuning fork of frequency 50 Hz produces sound of wavelength 6.6 m in air. Calculate the speed of sound.","steps":[{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪v=f\\lambda⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪v=50\\times 6.6⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪v=330\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫"}],"answer":"330 m s^−1^"},{"id":"week07.7.2.e2","title":"Wavelength of a radio wave","question":"A radio station broadcasts at 100 MHz. Radio waves travel at ⟪3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫. Calculate the wavelength.","steps":[{"label":"Convert","text":"⟪f=100\\,\\mathrm{MHz}=100\\times 10^{6}=1.00\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫"},{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪\\lambda =v/f⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪\\lambda =3.0\\times 10^{8}\\div 1.00\\times 10^{8}⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪\\lambda =3.0\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫"}],"answer":"3.0 m"},{"id":"week07.7.2.e3","title":"Period and frequency","question":"The period of a wave is 0.02 s. Find its frequency.","steps":[{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪f=1/T⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪f=1\\div 0.02⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪f=50\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫"}],"answer":"50 Hz"},{"id":"week07.7.2.e4","title":"Counting ripples","question":"In a ripple tank, 12 complete waves pass a point in 4.0 s. The distance between neighbouring crests is 3.0 cm. Calculate (a) the frequency, (b) the period and (c) the speed of the ripples.","steps":[{"label":"(a) Frequency","text":"f = number of waves ÷ time ⟪=12\\div 4.0=3.0\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫"},{"label":"(b) Period","text":"⟪T=1/f=1\\div 3.0=0.33\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫"},{"label":"(c) Convert","text":"⟪\\lambda =3.0\\,\\mathrm{cm}=0.030\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫"},{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪v=f\\lambda =3.0\\times 0.030⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪v=0.090\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}\\ (=9.0\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1})⟫"}],"answer":"(a) 3.0 Hz  (b) 0.33 s  (c) 0.090 m s^−1^"}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week07.7.2.p1","kind":"self","q":"Define (a) **amplitude** and (b) **frequency**.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"(a) Maximum displacement of a particle from its rest position (1). (b) Number of complete waves/oscillations per second (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) Maximum displacement of a particle from its rest position","marks":1},{"text":"(b) Number of complete waves/oscillations per second","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Speed of a sound wave). Start like this: Formula: ⟪v=f\\lambda⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week07.7.2.p2","kind":"self","q":"Define **wavelength**.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"Distance between two successive points in phase, e.g. crest to next crest (1).","points":[{"text":"Distance between two successive points in phase, e.g. crest to next crest","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Speed of a sound wave). Start like this: Formula: ⟪v=f\\lambda⟫","from":1}}],"practice":[{"id":"week07.7.2.p3","kind":"num","q":"A wave has a frequency of 25 Hz. Calculate its period.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪T=1/f=1/25⟫ (1) ⟪=0.04\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪T=1/f=1/25⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=0.04\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":0.04,"sf":1,"unit":"s","unitTex":"\\mathrm{s}"},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Speed of a sound wave). Start like this: Formula: ⟪v=f\\lambda⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week07.7.2.p4","kind":"num","q":"A wave on a rope has frequency 4.0 Hz and wavelength 0.50 m. Calculate its speed.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪v=f\\lambda =4.0\\times 0.50⟫ (1) ⟪=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪v=f\\lambda =4.0\\times 0.50⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":2,"sf":2,"unit":"m s^−1^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}"},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Speed of a sound wave). Start like this: Formula: ⟪v=f\\lambda⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week07.7.2.p5","kind":"num","q":"Sound travels at 340 m s^−1^ in air. Calculate the wavelength of a note of frequency 680 Hz.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪\\lambda =v/f=340/680⟫ (1) ⟪=0.50\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪\\lambda =v/f=340/680⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=0.50\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":0.5,"sf":2,"unit":"m","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}"},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Speed of a sound wave). Start like this: Formula: ⟪v=f\\lambda⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week07.7.2.p6","kind":"num","q":"A water wave has speed 1.5 m s^−1^ and wavelength 30 cm. Calculate its frequency.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪\\lambda =0.30\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (1); ⟪f=v/\\lambda =1.5/0.30⟫ (1) ⟪=5.0\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪\\lambda =0.30\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪f=v/\\lambda =1.5/0.30⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=5.0\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":5,"sf":2,"unit":"Hz","unitTex":"\\mathrm{Hz}"},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Speed of a sound wave). Start like this: Formula: ⟪v=f\\lambda⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week07.7.2.p7","kind":"self","q":"The distance from a crest to the next trough of a wave is 0.60 m, and the vertical height from crest to trough is 0.20 m. State the wavelength and the amplitude.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Crest to next trough = half a wavelength, so ⟪\\lambda =1.2\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (1); amplitude ⟪=0.20\\div 2=0.10\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"Crest to next trough = half a wavelength, so ⟪\\lambda =1.2\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"amplitude ⟪=0.20\\div 2=0.10\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Wavelength of a radio wave). Start like this: Convert: ⟪f=100\\,\\mathrm{MHz}=100\\times 10^{6}=1.00\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫","from":2}}],"exit":[{"id":"week07.7.2.p8","kind":"self","q":"Sound of frequency 500 Hz passes from air ⟪(v=330\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1})⟫ into water ⟪(v=1500\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1})⟫. (a) State what happens to the frequency. (b) Calculate the wavelength in air and in water.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":4,"scheme":"(a) Frequency stays the same, 500 Hz (1). (b) Air: ⟪\\lambda =330/500⟫ (1) ⟪=0.66\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (1); water: ⟪\\lambda =1500/500=3.0\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) Frequency stays the same, 500 Hz","marks":1},{"text":"(b) Air: ⟪\\lambda =330/500⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=0.66\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"water: ⟪\\lambda =1500/500=3.0\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫","marks":1}],"section":"exit"},{"id":"week07.7.2.p9","kind":"num","q":"A microwave oven uses waves of frequency 2.45 GHz (speed ⟪3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫). Calculate the wavelength.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪f=2.45\\times 10^{9}\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫ (1); ⟪\\lambda =3.0\\times 10^{8}/2.45\\times 10^{9}⟫ (1) ⟪=0.12\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪f=2.45\\times 10^{9}\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪\\lambda =3.0\\times 10^{8}/2.45\\times 10^{9}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=0.12\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":0.12,"sf":2,"unit":"m","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}"},"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week07.7.2.w1","kind":"self","q":"What is the difference between a **pulse** and a **progressive wave**?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Pulse: a single disturbance (1). Progressive wave: a continuous series of disturbances from a source that keeps vibrating (1).","points":[{"text":"Pulse: a single disturbance","marks":1},{"text":"Progressive wave: a continuous series of disturbances from a source that keeps vibrating","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week07.7.1.p2"},{"id":"week07.7.2.w2","kind":"self","q":"Define a **wave**.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"A disturbance that transfers energy (1) from one place to another without transferring matter / the medium (1).","points":[{"text":"A disturbance that transfers energy","marks":1},{"text":"from one place to another without transferring matter / the medium","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week07.7.1.p1"},{"id":"week07.7.2.w3","kind":"self","q":"Define a **longitudinal** wave and give ONE example.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Particles vibrate parallel to (along) the direction of travel (1); e.g. sound / ultrasound / push–pull wave on a slinky (1).","points":[{"text":"Particles vibrate parallel to (along) the direction of travel","marks":1},{"text":"e.g. sound / ultrasound / push–pull wave on a slinky","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week07.7.1.p4"}],"n":48},{"id":"week07.7.3","kind":"unit","unit":"7.3","title":"Wave Graphs: Displacement–Position and Displacement–Time","titleText":"Wave Graphs: Displacement–Position and Displacement–Time","goals":[{"code":"C1.3","text":"Draw and interpret displacement–position and displacement–time graphs of a wave"}],"codes":["C1.3"],"words":[{"term":"Wave","def":"A disturbance that transfers energy without transferring matter."},{"term":"Medium","def":"The material a wave travels through."},{"term":"Amplitude","def":"Maximum displacement from the rest position."},{"term":"Wavelength","def":"Distance between successive points in phase (crest to crest)."},{"term":"Frequency","def":"Number of complete waves per second (Hz)."},{"term":"Period","def":"Time for one complete wave (s)."},{"term":"CRO","def":"Cathode ray oscilloscope — displays a wave as a trace on a screen."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"p","text":"CSEC uses two graphs that LOOK the same (both are wavy) but tell you different things. The ONLY way to know which is which is to **read the x-axis label**."},{"type":"h","text":"1. Displacement–position (displacement–distance) graph"},{"type":"bullets","items":["It is like a **photograph** of the whole wave at ONE instant.","x-axis = distance along the wave (position).","From it you read: **wavelength λ** (horizontal distance for one full cycle) and **amplitude a** (height of a crest above the middle line)."]},{"type":"img","src":"img/week07/wave_dp","alt":"Figure 7.5 — Displacement–position graph: gives λ and a.","caption":"Figure 7.5 — Displacement–position graph: gives λ and a.","width":5,"w":1214,"h":654},{"type":"h","text":"2. Displacement–time graph"},{"type":"bullets","items":["It follows **ONE particle** of the medium as time passes (like a video of one point).","x-axis = time.","From it you read: **period T** (time for one full cycle) and **amplitude a**. Then ⟪f=1/T⟫."]},{"type":"img","src":"img/week07/wave_dt","alt":"Figure 7.6 — Displacement–time graph: gives T (and hence f) and a.","caption":"Figure 7.6 — Displacement–time graph: gives T (and hence f) and a.","width":5,"w":1214,"h":614},{"type":"table","headers":["","Displacement–position graph","Displacement–time graph"],"rows":[["x-axis","distance / position (m or cm)","time (s or ms)"],["Shows","the whole wave at one instant","one particle over a period of time"],["One full cycle on the x-axis = ","wavelength λ","period T"],["Peak height = ","amplitude a","amplitude a"],["To get the speed","need f from somewhere else; ⟪v=f\\lambda⟫","need λ from somewhere else; ⟪v=\\lambda /T⟫"]]},{"type":"tip","text":"To read one full cycle accurately, measure across SEVERAL cycles and divide. E.g. if 3 cycles take 24 ms, ⟪T=24\\div 3=8\\,\\mathrm{ms}⟫."},{"type":"tip","text":"If you have BOTH graphs for the same wave: λ from the position graph, T from the time graph, then ⟪v=\\lambda /T⟫ (same as ⟪v=f\\lambda⟫)."},{"type":"h","text":"Drawing a wave graph in an exam"},{"type":"steps","items":["Draw and label both axes with quantity and unit (e.g. displacement / cm; time / ms).","Mark the amplitude on the y-axis (e.g. +3 and −3).","Mark one cycle on the x-axis using λ (position graph) or T (time graph).","Draw a smooth, symmetrical curve: zero → crest → zero → trough → zero for each cycle."]},{"type":"warn","text":"Writing 'wavelength' when the x-axis says time. A cycle on a time axis is the PERIOD.","label":"Common mistake"}],"examples":[{"id":"week07.7.3.e1","title":"Reading a displacement–position graph","question":"The graph shows a wave on a string at one instant. Find (a) the amplitude and (b) the wavelength.","img":{"src":"img/week07/dp_numbers","alt":"The graph shows a wave on a string at one instant. Find (a) the amplitude and (b) the wavelength.","w":1174,"h":574},"steps":[{"label":"(a)","text":"Highest point is at ⟪y=3.0\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫ → amplitude ⟪=3.0\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫"},{"label":"(b)","text":"Two full cycles fit between 0 and ⟪80\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\rightarrow \\lambda =80\\div 2=40\\,\\mathrm{cm}=0.40\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫"}],"answer":"⟪a=3.0\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫;  ⟪\\lambda =40\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\ (0.40\\,\\mathrm{m})⟫"},{"id":"week07.7.3.e2","title":"Reading a displacement–time graph","question":"The graph shows how the displacement of ONE point on the same string changes with time. Find (a) the period, (b) the frequency and (c) using the previous example, the speed of the wave.","img":{"src":"img/week07/dt_numbers","alt":"The graph shows how the displacement of ONE point on the same string changes with time. Find (a) the period, (b) the frequency and (c) using the previous example, the speed of the wave.","w":1174,"h":574},"steps":[{"label":"(a) Period","text":"One cycle from 0 to 8 ms (2.5 cycles in 20 ms: 20 ÷ 2.5 = 8 ms) → T = 8 ms = 0.008 s"},{"label":"(b) Frequency","text":"⟪f=1/T=1\\div 0.008=125\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫"},{"label":"(c) Formula","text":"⟪v=f\\lambda⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪v=125\\times 0.40⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪v=50\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫"}],"answer":"⟪T=8\\,\\mathrm{ms}⟫;  ⟪f=125\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫;  ⟪v=50\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫"},{"id":"week07.7.3.e3","title":"Comparing two waves","question":"The graph shows two sound waves P and Q. Compare their amplitudes and frequencies.","img":{"src":"img/week07/two_waves","alt":"The graph shows two sound waves P and Q. Compare their amplitudes and frequencies.","w":1174,"h":574},"steps":[{"label":"Amplitudes","text":"P: 2 mm; Q: 1 mm → P has twice the amplitude of Q."},{"label":"Periods","text":"P: one cycle takes 4 ms; Q: one cycle takes 2 ms."},{"label":"Frequencies","text":"⟪f_{P}=1\\div 0.004=250\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫;  ⟪f_{Q}=1\\div 0.002=500\\,\\mathrm{Hz}\\rightarrow Q⟫ has twice the frequency of P."}],"answer":"P: ⟪a=2\\,\\mathrm{mm}⟫, ⟪f=250\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫.  Q: ⟪a=1\\,\\mathrm{mm}⟫, ⟪f=500\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫."}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week07.7.3.p1","kind":"self","q":"State what quantity can be read from the x-axis of (a) a displacement–position graph and (b) a displacement–time graph for one complete cycle.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"(a) Wavelength (1). (b) Period (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) Wavelength","marks":1},{"text":"(b) Period","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Reading a displacement–position graph). Start like this: (a): Highest point is at ⟪y=3.0\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫ → amplitude ⟪=3.0\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week07.7.3.p2","kind":"num","q":"Use the displacement–position graph in Worked Example 7.3.1. State the displacement of the string at ⟪x=30\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"−3.0 cm (a trough) (1).","points":[{"text":"−3.0 cm (a trough)","marks":1}],"num":{"value":-3,"sf":2,"unit":"cm","unitTex":"\\mathrm{cm}"},"conf":"low","section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Reading a displacement–position graph). Start like this: (a): Highest point is at ⟪y=3.0\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫ → amplitude ⟪=3.0\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫","from":1}}],"practice":[{"id":"week07.7.3.p3","kind":"self","q":"Use the displacement–time graph in Worked Example 7.3.2. How many complete oscillations does the point make in 20 ms?","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"⟪20\\div 8=2.5⟫ oscillations (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪20\\div 8=2.5⟫ oscillations","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Reading a displacement–time graph). Start like this: (a) Period: One cycle from 0 to 8 ms (2.5 cycles in 20 ms: 20 ÷ 2.5 = 8 ms) → T = 8 ms = 0.008 s","from":2}},{"id":"week07.7.3.p4","kind":"self","q":"A displacement–time graph shows 5 complete cycles in 0.10 s. Calculate the period and the frequency.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪T=0.10\\div 5=0.020\\,\\mathrm{s}\\ (1)⟫(1); ⟪f=1/0.020=50\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪T=0.10\\div 5=0.020\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫","marks":2},{"text":"⟪f=1/0.020=50\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Reading a displacement–time graph). Start like this: (a) Period: One cycle from 0 to 8 ms (2.5 cycles in 20 ms: 20 ÷ 2.5 = 8 ms) → T = 8 ms = 0.008 s","from":2}},{"id":"week07.7.3.p5","kind":"num","q":"A displacement–position graph shows 4 complete waves in 2.0 m. The frequency of the wave is 6.0 Hz. Calculate the wave speed.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪\\lambda =2.0\\div 4=0.50\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (1); ⟪v=f\\lambda =6.0\\times 0.50⟫ (1) ⟪=3.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪\\lambda =2.0\\div 4=0.50\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪v=f\\lambda =6.0\\times 0.50⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=3.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":3,"sf":2,"unit":"m s^−1^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}"},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Reading a displacement–time graph). Start like this: (a) Period: One cycle from 0 to 8 ms (2.5 cycles in 20 ms: 20 ÷ 2.5 = 8 ms) → T = 8 ms = 0.008 s","from":2}}],"exit":[{"id":"week07.7.3.p6","kind":"self","q":"In the space below, sketch a displacement–time graph for a wave of amplitude 2 cm and period 4 s, for 0 to 8 s. Label both axes.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":6,"scheme":"Axes labelled displacement / cm and time / s (1); peaks at +2 and troughs at −2 (1); one cycle every 4 s, crossings at 0, 2, 4, 6, 8 s (1); smooth sinusoidal shape showing 2 cycles (1).","points":[{"text":"Axes labelled displacement / cm and time / s","marks":1},{"text":"peaks at +2 and troughs at −2","marks":1},{"text":"one cycle every 4 s, crossings at 0, 2, 4, 6, 8 s","marks":1},{"text":"smooth sinusoidal shape showing 2 cycles","marks":1}],"section":"exit"},{"id":"week07.7.3.p7","kind":"self","q":"Two waves R and S are drawn on the same displacement–time axes. R has twice the period of S and the same amplitude. Which has the higher frequency? Explain.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"S (1): ⟪f=1/T⟫, so a smaller period means a higher frequency (S has twice the frequency of R) (1).","points":[{"text":"S","marks":1},{"text":": ⟪f=1/T⟫, so a smaller period means a higher frequency (S has twice the frequency of R)","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week07.7.3.w1","kind":"self","q":"Define **wavelength**.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"Distance between two successive points in phase, e.g. crest to next crest (1).","points":[{"text":"Distance between two successive points in phase, e.g. crest to next crest","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week07.7.2.p2"},{"id":"week07.7.3.w2","kind":"num","q":"A wave has a frequency of 25 Hz. Calculate its period.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪T=1/f=1/25⟫ (1) ⟪=0.04\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪T=1/f=1/25⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=0.04\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":0.04,"sf":1,"unit":"s","unitTex":"\\mathrm{s}"},"section":"warmup","src":"week07.7.2.p3"},{"id":"week07.7.3.w3","kind":"num","q":"A wave on a rope has frequency 4.0 Hz and wavelength 0.50 m. Calculate its speed.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪v=f\\lambda =4.0\\times 0.50⟫ (1) ⟪=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪v=f\\lambda =4.0\\times 0.50⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":2,"sf":2,"unit":"m s^−1^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}"},"section":"warmup","src":"week07.7.2.p4"}],"n":49},{"id":"week07.7.4","kind":"unit","unit":"7.4","title":"Sound: How It Is Made, How It Travels, Pitch and Loudness","titleText":"Sound: How It Is Made, How It Travels, Pitch and Loudness","goals":[{"code":"C2.1","text":"Explain how sound is produced by vibrating sources and why sound needs a material medium to travel"},{"code":"C2.2","text":"Relate pitch to frequency and loudness to amplitude"}],"codes":["C2.1","C2.2"],"words":[{"term":"Wave","def":"A disturbance that transfers energy without transferring matter."},{"term":"Medium","def":"The material a wave travels through."},{"term":"Compression","def":"Region of a longitudinal wave where particles are close together."},{"term":"Rarefaction","def":"Region of a longitudinal wave where particles are spread out."},{"term":"Amplitude","def":"Maximum displacement from the rest position."},{"term":"Wavelength","def":"Distance between successive points in phase (crest to crest)."},{"term":"Frequency","def":"Number of complete waves per second (Hz)."},{"term":"Period","def":"Time for one complete wave (s)."},{"term":"Pitch","def":"How high or low a note sounds; depends on frequency."},{"term":"Loudness","def":"How loud a sound is; depends on amplitude."},{"term":"Ultrasound","def":"Sound with frequency above 20 kHz."},{"term":"CRO","def":"Cathode ray oscilloscope — displays a wave as a trace on a screen."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"h","text":"Sound is produced by vibrations"},{"type":"p","text":"**Every sound is made by something vibrating.** Touch your throat while you hum — you feel your vocal cords vibrating."},{"type":"table","headers":["Source","What vibrates"],"rows":[["Guitar / steel pan / piano","Strings or the metal surface of the pan"],["Drum","The stretched skin"],["Voice","Vocal cords"],["Loudspeaker","The paper/plastic cone"],["Flute / bottle","The column of air inside"],["Tuning fork","The prongs (dip a sounding fork in water — it splashes)"]]},{"type":"h","text":"How sound travels"},{"type":"bullets","items":["The vibrating source pushes on the air molecules next to it, squashing them together → a **compression**.","As it moves back it leaves a region where molecules are spread out → a **rarefaction**.","Each layer of molecules pushes the next, so compressions and rarefactions travel outward. The molecules only vibrate back and forth **along** the direction of travel → sound is a **longitudinal** wave.","Because sound is passed on by particles, it **needs a material medium** (solid, liquid or gas). It **cannot travel through a vacuum**."]},{"type":"h","text":"Experiment: sound needs a medium (the bell-jar experiment)"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week07/bell_jar","alt":"Figure 7.7 — Electric bell in a bell jar connected to a vacuum pump.","caption":"Figure 7.7 — Electric bell in a bell jar connected to a vacuum pump.","width":4.6,"w":1054,"h":690},{"type":"steps","items":["**Apparatus:** electric bell, battery, glass bell jar on a flat plate, vacuum pump.","Switch on the bell. Note that you can hear it ringing and see the hammer striking the gong.","Switch on the vacuum pump to remove the air from the jar gradually.","**Observation:** the sound gets fainter and fainter until it can hardly be heard, but the hammer can still be **seen** striking the gong.","Let air back into the jar: the sound becomes loud again.","**Conclusion:** sound needs a material medium to travel; it cannot travel through a vacuum. Light CAN travel through a vacuum (we still see the bell)."]},{"type":"tip","text":"Why can a faint sound still be heard? The pump never removes ALL the air, and some sound is carried through the wires and the base plate (solids). Suspend the bell from rubber bands / place it on foam to reduce this. This is a common 'precaution' mark."},{"type":"h","text":"Speed of sound in different media"},{"type":"table","headers":["Medium","Approximate speed of sound / m s^−1^"],"rows":[["Air (about 20 °C)","330 – 340"],["Water","1500"],["Steel","5000 – 6000"],["Vacuum","0 — sound cannot travel"]]},{"type":"bullets","items":["Sound travels **fastest in solids**, slower in liquids and slowest in gases (particles are closest together in solids).","Sound travels faster in **warmer** air.","Light is about a million times faster than sound ⟪(3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1})⟫."]},{"type":"h","text":"Pitch, loudness and the range of hearing"},{"type":"def","term":"Pitch","text":"how high or low a note sounds. Pitch depends on **frequency**: higher frequency → higher pitch."},{"type":"def","term":"Loudness","text":"how loud a sound is. Loudness depends on **amplitude**: larger amplitude → louder sound (more energy)."},{"type":"img","src":"img/week07/cro_pitch_loud","alt":"Figure 7.8 — Sound waves shown on a cathode ray oscilloscope (CRO) screen.","caption":"Figure 7.8 — Sound waves shown on a cathode ray oscilloscope (CRO) screen.","width":5.6,"w":1254,"h":754},{"type":"p","text":"A microphone connected to a **cathode ray oscilloscope (CRO)** turns a sound wave into a picture like a displacement–time graph. Taller trace = louder. More waves squeezed across the screen = higher pitch."},{"type":"table","headers":["Change to the note","What happens to the trace on the CRO"],"rows":[["Louder (same note)","Waves get TALLER (amplitude up); same number of waves across the screen"],["Higher pitch (same loudness)","MORE waves across the screen (closer together); same height"],["Softer and lower","Shorter AND fewer waves"]]},{"type":"def","term":"Audible range (human hearing)","text":"about **20 Hz to 20 000 Hz (20 kHz)**. The upper limit falls with age."},{"type":"def","term":"Ultrasound","text":"sound with frequency **above 20 kHz** — too high for humans to hear."},{"type":"def","term":"Infrasound","text":"sound with frequency below 20 Hz (e.g. from earthquakes, elephants)."},{"type":"p","text":"Notes of the same pitch and loudness from different instruments sound different because their waveforms have different **shapes** — this is called the **quality** or **timbre** of the sound."},{"type":"warn","text":"Do not say 'pitch depends on amplitude' or 'louder means faster'. All sounds in air travel at the SAME speed whatever their pitch or loudness.","label":"Common mistake"}],"examples":[{"id":"week07.7.4.e1","title":"Pitch and loudness from a graph","question":"Using the graph of P and Q in Worked Example 7.3.3, state which sound (a) is louder and (b) has the higher pitch. Give reasons.","steps":[{"label":"(a)","text":"P is louder because it has the larger amplitude (2 mm vs 1 mm)."},{"label":"(b)","text":"Q has the higher pitch because it has the higher frequency (500 Hz vs 250 Hz)."}],"answer":"P louder (larger amplitude); Q higher pitch (higher frequency)"},{"id":"week07.7.4.e2","title":"Using a CRO screen","question":"The CRO trace shown is produced by a note. The time-base is set so that each horizontal division (square) represents 2.5 ms. Calculate (a) the period, (b) the frequency and (c) the wavelength of the note in air (speed 330 m s^−1^).","img":{"src":"img/week07/cro_screen","alt":"The CRO trace shown is produced by a note. The time-base is set so that each horizontal division (square) represents 2.5 ms. Calculate (a) the period, (b) the frequency and (c) the wavelength of the note in air (speed 330 m s−1).","w":894,"h":718},"steps":[{"label":"(a) Period","text":"One cycle covers 4 divisions → ⟪T=4\\times 2.5\\,\\mathrm{ms}=10\\,\\mathrm{ms}=0.010\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫"},{"label":"(b) Frequency","text":"⟪f=1/T=1\\div 0.010=100\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫"},{"label":"(c) Formula","text":"⟪\\lambda =v/f⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪\\lambda =330\\div 100⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪\\lambda =3.3\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫"}],"answer":"⟪T=10\\,\\mathrm{ms}⟫;  ⟪f=100\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫;  ⟪\\lambda =3.3\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫"}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week07.7.4.p1","kind":"self","q":"State what is common to all sources of sound.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"They are all vibrating (1).","points":[{"text":"They are all vibrating","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Pitch and loudness from a graph). Start like this: (a): P is louder because it has the larger amplitude (2 mm vs 1 mm).","from":1}},{"id":"week07.7.4.p2","kind":"self","q":"Explain, in terms of particles, how sound travels through air.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"Source vibrates and pushes/pulls on air molecules (1); creates compressions and rarefactions (1); molecules vibrate back and forth along the direction of travel, passing the energy on to neighbouring molecules (1).","points":[{"text":"Source vibrates and pushes/pulls on air molecules","marks":1},{"text":"creates compressions and rarefactions","marks":1},{"text":"molecules vibrate back and forth along the direction of travel, passing the energy on to neighbouring molecules","marks":1}],"section":"try"}],"practice":[{"id":"week07.7.4.p3","kind":"self","q":"Describe an experiment to show that sound cannot travel through a vacuum. Include what you would observe.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":6,"scheme":"Ringing electric bell inside a bell jar connected to a vacuum pump (1); air pumped out gradually (1); sound becomes fainter / almost inaudible (1); but the hammer is still seen moving, so sound needs a medium while light does not (1).","points":[{"text":"Ringing electric bell inside a bell jar connected to a vacuum pump","marks":1},{"text":"air pumped out gradually","marks":1},{"text":"sound becomes fainter / almost inaudible","marks":1},{"text":"but the hammer is still seen moving, so sound needs a medium while light does not","marks":1}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week07.7.4.p4","kind":"self","q":"Astronauts on the Moon (no air) cannot talk to each other directly but can talk by radio. Explain.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Sound needs a medium and there is no air on the Moon (1); radio waves are EM waves, which can travel through a vacuum (1).","points":[{"text":"Sound needs a medium and there is no air on the Moon","marks":1},{"text":"radio waves are EM waves, which can travel through a vacuum","marks":1}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week07.7.4.p5","kind":"self","q":"State the factor of a sound wave that determines (a) its pitch and (b) its loudness.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"(a) Frequency (1). (b) Amplitude (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) Frequency","marks":1},{"text":"(b) Amplitude","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Pitch and loudness from a graph). Start like this: (a): P is louder because it has the larger amplitude (2 mm vs 1 mm).","from":1}},{"id":"week07.7.4.p6","kind":"self","q":"State the audible frequency range for a healthy young person. What is ultrasound?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"20 Hz to 20 000 Hz / 20 kHz (1). Sound of frequency above 20 kHz (1).","points":[{"text":"20 Hz to 20 000 Hz / 20 kHz","marks":1},{"text":"Sound of frequency above 20 kHz","marks":1}],"section":"practice"}],"exit":[{"id":"week07.7.4.p7","kind":"self","q":"A note is displayed on a CRO. Describe how the trace changes when (a) the note is played more loudly at the same pitch and (b) a note of higher pitch is played at the same loudness.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"(a) Waves taller (bigger amplitude), same spacing (1). (b) More waves across the screen / waves closer together, same height (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) Waves taller (bigger amplitude), same spacing","marks":1},{"text":"(b) More waves across the screen / waves closer together, same height","marks":1}],"section":"exit"},{"id":"week07.7.4.p8","kind":"num","q":"On a CRO the time-base is 5 ms per division. One complete wave covers 2 divisions. Calculate the frequency of the sound.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪T=2\\times 5=10\\,\\mathrm{ms}=0.010\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫ (1); ⟪f=1/T⟫ (1) ⟪=100\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪T=2\\times 5=10\\,\\mathrm{ms}=0.010\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪f=1/T⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=100\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":100,"sf":1,"unit":"Hz","unitTex":"\\mathrm{Hz}"},"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week07.7.4.w1","kind":"self","q":"Use the displacement–time graph in Worked Example 7.3.2. How many complete oscillations does the point make in 20 ms?","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"⟪20\\div 8=2.5⟫ oscillations (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪20\\div 8=2.5⟫ oscillations","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week07.7.3.p3"},{"id":"week07.7.4.w2","kind":"num","q":"Use the displacement–position graph in Worked Example 7.3.1. State the displacement of the string at ⟪x=30\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"−3.0 cm (a trough) (1).","points":[{"text":"−3.0 cm (a trough)","marks":1}],"num":{"value":-3,"sf":2,"unit":"cm","unitTex":"\\mathrm{cm}"},"conf":"low","section":"warmup","src":"week07.7.3.p2"}],"n":50},{"id":"week07.7.5","kind":"unit","unit":"7.5","title":"Speed of Sound, Echoes, Wave Behaviour of Sound and Ultrasound","titleText":"Speed of Sound, Echoes, Wave Behaviour of Sound and Ultrasound","goals":[{"code":"C2.3","text":"Determine the speed of sound in air and use it in practical situations (echoes, thunder and lightning)"},{"code":"C2.4","text":"Describe evidence that sound can be reflected, refracted, diffracted and can show interference"},{"code":"C2.5","text":"Describe uses of ultrasound"}],"codes":["C2.3","C2.4","C2.5"],"words":[{"term":"Wave","def":"A disturbance that transfers energy without transferring matter."},{"term":"Medium","def":"The material a wave travels through."},{"term":"Pulse","def":"A single disturbance travelling through a medium."},{"term":"Compression","def":"Region of a longitudinal wave where particles are close together."},{"term":"Rarefaction","def":"Region of a longitudinal wave where particles are spread out."},{"term":"Wavelength","def":"Distance between successive points in phase (crest to crest)."},{"term":"Echo","def":"A reflected sound."},{"term":"Diffraction","def":"Spreading of waves through a gap or round an edge."},{"term":"Interference","def":"Two overlapping waves adding (loud/bright) or cancelling (quiet/dark)."},{"term":"Ultrasound","def":"Sound with frequency above 20 kHz."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"h","text":"Echoes"},{"type":"def","term":"Echo","text":"a sound **reflected** from a hard surface (e.g. a wall or cliff) that is heard a short time after the original sound."},{"type":"p","text":"In an echo, the sound travels **to** the wall **and back**, so the distance it travels is **2d**, where d is the distance to the wall."},{"type":"formula","tex":"v=2d/t\\qquad (\\text{so }d=vt/2)","where":["⟪v⟫ = speed of sound (m s^−1^)","⟪d⟫ = distance from the person to the reflecting surface (m)","⟪t⟫ = time between making the sound and hearing the echo (s)"],"label":"Echo formula"},{"type":"warn","text":"The most common mistake in echo questions is forgetting the 2. The sound goes THERE and BACK.","label":"Common mistake"},{"type":"h","text":"Experiment: measuring the speed of sound by the echo method"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week07/echo_clap","alt":"Figure 7.9 — Measuring the speed of sound using an echo from a tall wall.","caption":"Figure 7.9 — Measuring the speed of sound using an echo from a tall wall.","width":4.6,"w":775,"h":534},{"type":"steps","items":["**Apparatus:** two wooden blocks (or clap hands), stopwatch, long measuring tape, a large flat wall in an open field.","Stand at least 50 m from the wall (e.g. ⟪d=100\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫). Measure d with the tape.","Clap once and listen for the echo. Then clap steadily so that each clap is made **exactly when the echo of the previous clap is heard** (adjust the rhythm until clap and echo coincide).","A partner starts the stopwatch on one clap (counting it as 'zero') and stops it after 20 more claps. Record the time t~20~.","Time between claps ⟪=t_{20}\\div 20⟫. In this time the sound travels 2d.","Calculate ⟪v=2d\\div (t_{20}/20)⟫. Repeat 3 times and find the average."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Precaution","Reason"],"rows":[["Stand far from the wall ⟪(\\geq 50\\,\\mathrm{m})⟫","Longer echo time → smaller percentage error in timing (reaction time matters less)"],["Time many intervals (e.g. 20) and divide","Reduces the effect of reaction time"],["Repeat and average","More reliable result"],["Choose a calm day; no other walls/buildings nearby","Wind changes the speed; other surfaces give confusing echoes"]]},{"type":"p","text":"**Typical result and conclusion:** ⟪d=100\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫, 20 intervals in 12.2 s → interval ⟪=0.61\\,\\mathrm{s}\\rightarrow v=200\\div 0.61\\approx 330\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫. The speed of sound in air is about 330 m s^−1^."},{"type":"h","text":"Second method: flash-and-bang (two observers)"},{"type":"bullets","items":["Observer A fires a starting pistol (or bangs two blocks over the head) a measured distance (at least 500 m) from observer B.","B starts a stopwatch on seeing the **flash/smoke** (light arrives almost instantly) and stops it on hearing the **bang**.","⟪v=d/t⟫. Repeat with A and B swapping places and average to cancel the effect of wind."]},{"type":"h","text":"Thunder and lightning"},{"type":"p","text":"Lightning and thunder are made at the same moment. Light ⟪(3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1})⟫ reaches you almost instantly; sound takes about 3 s to travel each kilometre. So the time gap tells you how far away the storm is: ⟪\\boldsymbol{d=v\\times t}⟫ (NOT 2d — the sound travels one way only)."},{"type":"h","text":"Evidence that sound behaves like a wave (C2.4)"},{"type":"p","text":"All waves can be **reflected, refracted, diffracted** and can show **interference**. Water waves in a ripple tank show these clearly. Sound shows them too:"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week07/ripple_tank","alt":"Figure 7.10 — Wave behaviour seen in a ripple tank: (a) reflection, (b) refraction, (c) and (d) diffraction.","caption":"Figure 7.10 — Wave behaviour seen in a ripple tank: (a) reflection, (b) refraction, (c) and (d) diffraction.","width":5,"w":1129,"h":1094},{"type":"table","headers":["Wave property","What it means","Evidence for SOUND"],"rows":[["**Reflection**","Wave bounces off a surface","Echoes from walls and cliffs; sonar echoes from the sea bed; sound reflected by a curved dish"],["**Refraction**","Wave changes speed (and direction) when it enters a different medium","Sound heard more clearly at night over long distances: sound bends back towards the ground (Fig. 7.11). A balloon filled with carbon dioxide acts as a 'sound lens'"],["**Diffraction**","Wave spreads out after passing through a gap or round an edge — most when gap ≈ wavelength","You hear people talking round a corner or through an open door even when you cannot see them. Sound wavelengths (about 0.02 m – 17 m) are similar to door sizes, so sound diffracts a lot"],["**Interference**","Two waves overlap and add (loud) or cancel (quiet)","Two loudspeakers connected to the same signal generator: walking across in front of them you hear alternate LOUD and quiet regions (Fig. 7.12)"]]},{"type":"img","src":"img/week07/sound_refraction","alt":"Figure 7.11 — Refraction of sound: sound bends towards the cooler (slower) air.","caption":"Figure 7.11 — Refraction of sound: sound bends towards the cooler (slower) air.","width":5.6,"w":1253,"h":494},{"type":"img","src":"img/week07/two_speakers","alt":"Figure 7.12 — Interference of sound from two loudspeakers.","caption":"Figure 7.12 — Interference of sound from two loudspeakers.","width":4.8,"w":1048,"h":694},{"type":"tip","text":"Light diffracts very little round a door because its wavelength (about ⟪5\\times 10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫) is tiny compared with the gap. Sound's wavelength is about the size of the door — that is why you can HEAR round corners but not SEE round them."},{"type":"h","text":"Ultrasound and its uses (C2.5)"},{"type":"p","text":"Ultrasound is sound above 20 kHz. It reflects from boundaries between different materials, and because its wavelength is short it gives detailed echoes. It is also safe (non-ionising) for the human body."},{"type":"img","src":"img/week07/sonar","alt":"Figure 7.13 — Echo sounding (sonar): a ship measures the depth of the sea.","caption":"Figure 7.13 — Echo sounding (sonar): a ship measures the depth of the sea.","width":4.8,"w":1150,"h":614},{"type":"table","headers":["Use","How it works"],"rows":[["**Echo sounding / sonar**","A pulse is sent down from a ship; the time for the echo from the sea bed (or a shoal of fish / submarine) gives the depth: d = vt/2 (v ≈ 1500 m s^−1^ in sea water)"],["**Prenatal (pregnancy) scanning**","Echoes from tissue boundaries build up an image of the baby; safer than X-rays"],["**Medical imaging & treatment**","Scanning organs (liver, kidneys, heart); breaking up kidney stones; physiotherapy"],["**Cleaning**","Ultrasonic baths shake dirt off jewellery, watches, false teeth and surgical instruments"],["**Detecting cracks (flaws)**","In metal castings, aircraft parts and rails — an extra early echo reveals a crack"],["**Animals**","Bats and dolphins use ultrasound echoes to find their way and locate prey (echolocation)"]]}],"examples":[{"id":"week07.7.5.e1","title":"Echo from a wall","question":"A girl stands 99 m from a wall and claps. She hears the echo 0.60 s later. Calculate the speed of sound.","steps":[{"label":"Distance travelled","text":"⟪2d=2\\times 99=198\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫"},{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪v=2d/t⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪v=198\\div 0.60⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪v=330\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫"}],"answer":"330 m s^−1^"},{"id":"week07.7.5.e2","title":"Thunder and lightning","question":"A boy sees a flash of lightning and hears the thunder 3.0 s later. The speed of sound is 340 m s^−1^. How far away was the lightning?","steps":[{"label":"Idea","text":"Light arrives almost instantly. The sound travels ONE way only."},{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪d=v\\times t⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪d=340\\times 3.0⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪d=1020\\,\\mathrm{m}\\approx 1.0\\,\\mathrm{km}⟫"}],"answer":"1020 m (about 1.0 km)"},{"id":"week07.7.5.e3","title":"Depth of the sea (sonar)","question":"A ship sends an ultrasound pulse straight down. The echo returns 0.40 s later. The speed of sound in sea water is 1500 m s^−1^. Calculate the depth of the sea.","steps":[{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪d=vt/2⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪d=1500\\times 0.40\\div 2⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪d=300\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫"}],"answer":"300 m"},{"id":"week07.7.5.e4","title":"Clapping method","question":"A student stands 85 m from a wall and claps in time with the echoes. 20 intervals between claps take 10.0 s. Calculate the speed of sound.","steps":[{"label":"One interval","text":"⟪t=10.0\\div 20=0.50\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫"},{"label":"Distance per interval","text":"⟪2d=2\\times 85=170\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫"},{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪v=2d/t=170\\div 0.50⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪v=340\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫"}],"answer":"340 m s^−1^"}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week07.7.5.p1","kind":"self","q":"What is an echo?","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"A reflected sound (heard after the original sound) (1).","points":[{"text":"A reflected sound (heard after the original sound)","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Echo from a wall). Start like this: Distance travelled: ⟪2d=2\\times 99=198\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week07.7.5.p2","kind":"num","q":"A man stands 165 m from a cliff and shouts. The speed of sound is 330 m s^−1^. How long after shouting does he hear the echo?","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Distance ⟪=2\\times 165=330\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (1); ⟪t=d/v=330/330⟫ (1) ⟪=1.0\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"Distance ⟪=2\\times 165=330\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪t=d/v=330/330⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=1.0\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":1,"sf":2,"unit":"s","unitTex":"\\mathrm{s}"},"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Echo from a wall). Start like this: Distance travelled: ⟪2d=2\\times 99=198\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫","from":1}}],"practice":[{"id":"week07.7.5.p3","kind":"num","q":"Thunder is heard 5.0 s after the lightning flash. Taking the speed of sound as 340 m s^−1^, calculate the distance to the storm.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪d=vt=340\\times 5.0⟫ (1) ⟪=1700\\,\\mathrm{m}\\ (1.7\\,\\mathrm{km})⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪d=vt=340\\times 5.0⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=1700\\,\\mathrm{m}\\ (1.7\\,\\mathrm{km})⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":1700,"sf":2,"unit":"m","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}","alt":[1.7]},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Thunder and lightning). Start like this: Idea: Light arrives almost instantly. The sound travels ONE way only.","from":2}},{"id":"week07.7.5.p4","kind":"self","q":"Explain why we see the lightning before we hear the thunder.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Light travels much faster than sound (1) (⟪3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ compared with about 340 m s^−1^), so it arrives almost instantly (1).","points":[{"text":"Light travels much faster than sound","marks":1},{"text":"(⟪3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ compared with about 340 m s^−1^), so it arrives almost instantly","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Thunder and lightning). Start like this: Idea: Light arrives almost instantly. The sound travels ONE way only.","from":2}},{"id":"week07.7.5.p5","kind":"self","q":"In the echo method, give TWO precautions to obtain an accurate value of the speed of sound, with reasons.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":4,"scheme":"Any two with reasons: stand far from wall — longer time, smaller % timing error ⟪(1+1)⟫; time many (e.g. 20) intervals — reduces reaction-time error ⟪(1+1)⟫; repeat and average — reliability ⟪(1+1)⟫; calm day / no other reflecting surfaces — wind/other echoes ⟪(1+1)⟫.","points":[{"text":"Any two with reasons: stand far from wall — longer time, smaller % timing error","marks":2},{"text":"time many (e.g. 20) intervals — reduces reaction-time error","marks":2},{"text":"repeat and average — reliability","marks":2},{"text":"calm day / no other reflecting surfaces — wind/other echoes","marks":2}],"conf":"low","section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 4 (Clapping method). Start like this: One interval: ⟪t=10.0\\div 20=0.50\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫","from":4}},{"id":"week07.7.5.p6","kind":"num","q":"A ship's sonar receives an echo from the sea bed 0.80 s after the pulse is sent. The speed of sound in water is 1500 m s^−1^. Calculate the depth of the water.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪d=vt/2⟫ (1) ⟪=1500\\times 0.80/2⟫ (1) ⟪=600\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪d=vt/2⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=1500\\times 0.80/2⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=600\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":600,"sf":1,"unit":"m","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}"},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 3 (Depth of the sea (sonar)). Start like this: Formula: ⟪d=vt/2⟫","from":3}},{"id":"week07.7.5.p7","kind":"self","q":"Explain why you can hear someone talking in the next room through an open door, even though you cannot see them.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"Sound diffracts (spreads out) through the doorway (1); because the wavelength of sound is similar to the width of the door (1); light's wavelength is far smaller than the door so it hardly diffracts (1).","points":[{"text":"Sound diffracts (spreads out) through the doorway","marks":1},{"text":"because the wavelength of sound is similar to the width of the door","marks":1},{"text":"light's wavelength is far smaller than the door so it hardly diffracts","marks":1}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week07.7.5.p8","kind":"self","q":"Two loudspeakers connected to the same signal generator face a student. As the student walks across in front of them, the sound gets loud, soft, loud, soft. Name and explain this effect.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"Interference (1). Where waves from both speakers arrive in step they add → loud (1); where they arrive out of step (compression meets rarefaction) they cancel → quiet (1).","points":[{"text":"Interference","marks":1},{"text":"Where waves from both speakers arrive in step they add → loud","marks":1},{"text":"where they arrive out of step (compression meets rarefaction) they cancel → quiet","marks":1}],"section":"practice"}],"exit":[{"id":"week07.7.5.p9","kind":"self","q":"State THREE uses of ultrasound.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Any three: echo sounding/sonar; prenatal scanning/medical imaging; cleaning jewellery/instruments; detecting cracks in metal; breaking kidney stones; physiotherapy (1 each).","points":[{"text":"Any three: echo sounding/sonar; prenatal scanning/medical imaging; cleaning jewellery/instruments; detecting cracks in metal; breaking kidney stones; physiotherapy (1 each).","marks":3,"each":true}],"section":"exit"},{"id":"week07.7.5.p10","kind":"self","q":"Explain why ultrasound rather than X-rays is used to scan an unborn baby.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Ultrasound is non-ionising/does not damage cells (1); X-rays are ionising and can harm the developing baby (1).","points":[{"text":"Ultrasound is non-ionising/does not damage cells","marks":1},{"text":"X-rays are ionising and can harm the developing baby","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week07.7.5.w1","kind":"self","q":"State what is common to all sources of sound.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"They are all vibrating (1).","points":[{"text":"They are all vibrating","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week07.7.4.p1"},{"id":"week07.7.5.w2","kind":"self","q":"State the factor of a sound wave that determines (a) its pitch and (b) its loudness.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"(a) Frequency (1). (b) Amplitude (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) Frequency","marks":1},{"text":"(b) Amplitude","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week07.7.4.p5"},{"id":"week07.7.5.w3","kind":"self","q":"Astronauts on the Moon (no air) cannot talk to each other directly but can talk by radio. Explain.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Sound needs a medium and there is no air on the Moon (1); radio waves are EM waves, which can travel through a vacuum (1).","points":[{"text":"Sound needs a medium and there is no air on the Moon","marks":1},{"text":"radio waves are EM waves, which can travel through a vacuum","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week07.7.4.p4"}],"n":51},{"id":"week07.7.6","kind":"unit","unit":"7.6","title":"The Electromagnetic Spectrum","titleText":"The Electromagnetic Spectrum","goals":[{"code":"C3.1","text":"State the properties of electromagnetic waves"},{"code":"C3.2","text":"List the members of the electromagnetic spectrum in order of wavelength (or frequency)"},{"code":"C3.3","text":"Give a source and a use of each type of electromagnetic wave"}],"codes":["C3.1","C3.2","C3.3"],"words":[{"term":"Wave","def":"A disturbance that transfers energy without transferring matter."},{"term":"Medium","def":"The material a wave travels through."},{"term":"Transverse wave","def":"Particles vibrate at 90° to the direction of travel."},{"term":"Wavelength","def":"Distance between successive points in phase (crest to crest)."},{"term":"Frequency","def":"Number of complete waves per second (Hz)."},{"term":"Interference","def":"Two overlapping waves adding (loud/bright) or cancelling (quiet/dark)."},{"term":"Electromagnetic spectrum","def":"The family of EM waves: radio, microwaves, IR, visible, UV, X-rays, gamma."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"p","text":"Light is just one member of a big family of waves called **electromagnetic (EM) waves**. Radio waves, microwaves, X-rays and the rest are all the same kind of wave — they only differ in wavelength and frequency."},{"type":"def","term":"Electromagnetic wave","text":"a transverse wave made of **oscillating electric and magnetic fields** at right angles to each other, which can travel through a vacuum at ⟪3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫."},{"type":"h","text":"Properties of ALL electromagnetic waves (C3.1) — learn this list"},{"type":"steps","items":["They are **transverse** waves.","They all travel at the **same speed in a vacuum**: ⟪c=3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (they are slower in glass or water).","They **do not need a medium** — they can travel through a vacuum (e.g. light from the Sun through space).","They **transfer energy**.","They can be **reflected, refracted, diffracted** and show **interference**.","They obey the wave equation ⟪\\boldsymbol{c=f\\lambda }⟫.","They carry no charge, so they are **not deflected by electric or magnetic fields**."]},{"type":"formula","tex":"c=f\\lambda","where":["⟪c⟫ = speed of EM waves ⟪=3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (in a vacuum or air)","⟪f⟫ = frequency (Hz)","⟪\\lambda⟫ = wavelength (m)"]},{"type":"h","text":"The order of the spectrum (C3.2)"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week07/em_spectrum","alt":"Figure 7.14 — The electromagnetic spectrum (approximate wavelengths).","caption":"Figure 7.14 — The electromagnetic spectrum (approximate wavelengths).","width":6,"w":1000,"h":554},{"type":"p","text":"From **longest wavelength (lowest frequency, lowest energy)** to **shortest wavelength (highest frequency, highest energy)**:"},{"type":"remember","text":"**R**adio → **M**icrowaves → **I**nfrared → **V**isible → **U**ltraviolet → **X**-rays → **G**amma rays.  Memory aid: **R**aging **M**artians **I**nvaded **V**enus **U**sing **X**-ray **G**uns.","label":"Learn the order"},{"type":"bullets","items":["Because c is the same for all, a **longer wavelength means a lower frequency** ⟪(f=c/\\lambda )⟫.","Visible light runs from **red** (longest, about ⟪7\\times 10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫) to **violet** (shortest, about ⟪4\\times 10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫): ROYGBIV.","**Infra**red is just beyond red ('below red' in frequency); **ultra**violet is just beyond violet ('above violet')."]},{"type":"h","text":"Sources and uses of each type (C3.3)"},{"type":"table","headers":["Type","Source","Uses","Danger"],"rows":[["Radio waves","Oscillating electric currents in transmitter aerials; stars","Radio and TV broadcasting, two-way radios, communication","Very little"],["Microwaves","Magnetron (in ovens); transmitters; radar sets","Cooking (heats water in food), mobile phones, satellite communication, Wi-Fi, radar","Heating of body tissue"],["Infrared (IR)","ALL warm and hot objects; the Sun; heaters","Remote controls, heaters/grills/toasters, thermal imaging and night-vision cameras, physiotherapy","Burns to skin"],["Visible light","The Sun, lamps, flames, lasers, LEDs","Seeing, photography, optical fibres (endoscopes, internet), photosynthesis","Very bright light/lasers damage eyes"],["Ultraviolet (UV)","The Sun, mercury-vapour lamps, very hot objects, welding arcs","Sterilising water and equipment (kills bacteria), detecting forged bank notes (fluorescence), sun-tanning, making vitamin D","Sunburn, skin cancer, eye damage"],["X-rays","X-ray tubes (fast electrons hitting a metal target)","Images of bones and teeth, airport baggage security, checking welds; radiotherapy","Ionising: damage cells, cause cancer"],["Gamma rays","Radioactive nuclei (radioactive decay)","Killing cancer cells (radiotherapy), sterilising medical instruments and food, tracers","Ionising: very harmful to cells"]]},{"type":"tip","text":"Exam questions often say 'State ONE source and ONE use of ...'. Give a specific answer: 'TV remote control' not 'communication'; 'X-ray tube' not 'machines'."},{"type":"warn","text":"Sound is NOT part of the EM spectrum. Sound is longitudinal and needs a medium; EM waves are transverse and do not.","label":"Common mistake"},{"type":"table","headers":["","Sound waves","Electromagnetic waves"],"rows":[["Type","Longitudinal","Transverse"],["Medium needed?","Yes — cannot travel in a vacuum","No — travel through a vacuum"],["Speed","About 330–340 m s^−1^ in air","⟪3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ in a vacuum"],["Made by","Vibrating objects","Oscillating electric charges / changes in atoms and nuclei"]]}],"examples":[{"id":"week07.7.6.e1","title":"Wavelength of a microwave","question":"A microwave oven produces waves of frequency 2.45 GHz. Calculate the wavelength.","steps":[{"label":"Convert","text":"⟪f=2.45\\,\\mathrm{GHz}=2.45\\times 10^{9}\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫"},{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪\\lambda =c/f⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪\\lambda =3.0\\times 10^{8}\\div 2.45\\times 10^{9}⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪\\lambda =0.12\\,\\mathrm{m}\\ (12\\,\\mathrm{cm})⟫"}],"answer":"0.12 m"},{"id":"week07.7.6.e2","title":"Frequency of an X-ray","question":"An X-ray has a wavelength of ⟪1.0\\times 10^{-10}\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫. Calculate its frequency.","steps":[{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪f=c/\\lambda⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪f=3.0\\times 10^{8}\\div 1.0\\times 10^{-10}⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪f=3.0\\times 10^{18}\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫"}],"answer":"⟪3.0\\times 10^{18}\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫"},{"id":"week07.7.6.e3","title":"Light from the Sun","question":"The Sun is ⟪1.5\\times 10^{11}\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ from the Earth. How long does sunlight take to reach us?","steps":[{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪t=d/c⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪t=1.5\\times 10^{11}\\div 3.0\\times 10^{8}⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪t=500\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫ (about 8.3 minutes)"}],"answer":"500 s"}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week07.7.6.p1","kind":"self","q":"State FOUR properties common to all electromagnetic waves.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":4,"scheme":"Any four: transverse; travel at ⟪3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ in vacuum; no medium needed/travel through vacuum; transfer energy; can be reflected/refracted/diffracted/interfere; obey ⟪c=f\\lambda⟫; not deflected by electric/magnetic fields (1 each).","points":[{"text":"Any four: transverse; travel at ⟪3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ in vacuum; no medium needed/travel through vacuum; transfer energy; can be reflected/refracted/diffracted/interfere; obey ⟪c=f\\lambda⟫; not deflected by electric/magnetic fields (1 each).","marks":4,"each":true}],"section":"try"},{"id":"week07.7.6.p2","kind":"self","q":"List the members of the electromagnetic spectrum in order of INCREASING wavelength.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Gamma rays, X-rays, ultraviolet, visible light, infrared, microwaves, radio waves (3 for fully correct order; −1 per error).","points":[{"text":"Gamma rays, X-rays, ultraviolet, visible light, infrared, microwaves, radio waves","marks":3}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Frequency of an X-ray). Start like this: Formula: ⟪f=c/\\lambda⟫","from":2}}],"practice":[{"id":"week07.7.6.p3","kind":"self","q":"Which member of the EM spectrum has (a) the highest frequency, (b) the longest wavelength?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"(a) Gamma rays (1). (b) Radio waves (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) Gamma rays","marks":1},{"text":"(b) Radio waves","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Frequency of an X-ray). Start like this: Formula: ⟪f=c/\\lambda⟫","from":2}},{"id":"week07.7.6.p4","kind":"self","q":"Name the EM wave used in each: (a) TV remote control, (b) sterilising surgical instruments, (c) detecting forged bank notes, (d) mobile phones.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":4,"scheme":"(a) Infrared (1). (b) Gamma rays (accept UV) (1). (c) Ultraviolet (1). (d) Microwaves (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) Infrared","marks":1},{"text":"(b) Gamma rays (accept UV)","marks":1},{"text":"(c) Ultraviolet","marks":1},{"text":"(d) Microwaves","marks":1}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week07.7.6.p5","kind":"self","q":"Give ONE source of (a) infrared radiation, (b) X-rays, (c) gamma rays.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"(a) Any hot/warm object, the Sun, heater (1). (b) X-ray tube (1). (c) Radioactive nuclei / radioactive substances (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) Any hot/warm object, the Sun, heater","marks":1},{"text":"(b) X-ray tube","marks":1},{"text":"(c) Radioactive nuclei / radioactive substances","marks":1}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week07.7.6.p6","kind":"num","q":"A radio station transmits on a wavelength of 1500 m. Calculate the frequency.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪f=c/\\lambda =3.0\\times 10^{8}/1500⟫ (1) ⟪=2.0\\times 10^{5}\\,\\mathrm{Hz}\\ (200\\,\\mathrm{kHz})⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪f=c/\\lambda =3.0\\times 10^{8}/1500⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=2.0\\times 10^{5}\\,\\mathrm{Hz}\\ (200\\,\\mathrm{kHz})⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":200000,"sf":2,"unit":"Hz","unitTex":"\\mathrm{Hz}","alt":[200]},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Frequency of an X-ray). Start like this: Formula: ⟪f=c/\\lambda⟫","from":2}}],"exit":[{"id":"week07.7.6.p7","kind":"num","q":"Yellow light has a wavelength of ⟪6.0\\times 10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫. Calculate its frequency.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪f=3.0\\times 10^{8}/6.0\\times 10^{-7}⟫ (1) ⟪=5.0\\times 10^{14}\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪f=3.0\\times 10^{8}/6.0\\times 10^{-7}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=5.0\\times 10^{14}\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":500000000000000,"sf":2,"unit":"Hz","unitTex":"\\mathrm{Hz}"},"section":"exit"},{"id":"week07.7.6.p8","kind":"self","q":"Give TWO differences between sound waves and light waves.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Any two: sound longitudinal, light transverse (1); sound needs a medium, light can travel in vacuum (1); light much faster (⟪3.0\\times 10^{8}vs⟫ about 340 m s^−1^) (1).","points":[{"text":"Any two: sound longitudinal, light transverse","marks":1},{"text":"sound needs a medium, light can travel in vacuum","marks":1},{"text":"light much faster (⟪3.0\\times 10^{8}vs⟫ about 340 m s^−1^)","marks":1}],"conf":"low","section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week07.7.6.w1","kind":"self","q":"Explain why we see the lightning before we hear the thunder.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Light travels much faster than sound (1) (⟪3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ compared with about 340 m s^−1^), so it arrives almost instantly (1).","points":[{"text":"Light travels much faster than sound","marks":1},{"text":"(⟪3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ compared with about 340 m s^−1^), so it arrives almost instantly","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week07.7.5.p4"},{"id":"week07.7.6.w2","kind":"self","q":"Explain why ultrasound rather than X-rays is used to scan an unborn baby.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Ultrasound is non-ionising/does not damage cells (1); X-rays are ionising and can harm the developing baby (1).","points":[{"text":"Ultrasound is non-ionising/does not damage cells","marks":1},{"text":"X-rays are ionising and can harm the developing baby","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week07.7.5.p10"},{"id":"week07.7.6.w3","kind":"self","q":"What is an echo?","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"A reflected sound (heard after the original sound) (1).","points":[{"text":"A reflected sound (heard after the original sound)","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week07.7.5.p1"}],"n":52},{"id":"week07.check","kind":"check","title":"Book check","titleText":"Book check","check":true,"watchOut":["Measuring amplitude from crest to trough (it is from the middle line to a crest — half that height).","Forgetting to convert kHz, MHz or GHz to Hz, or cm to m, before using ⟪v=f\\lambda⟫.","Calling one cycle on a displacement–TIME graph the 'wavelength' (it is the period).","Forgetting the 2 in echo questions ⟪(v=2d/t)⟫ — or wrongly using 2d for thunder and lightning (one way only).","Saying sound is transverse, or that sound can travel through space.","Saying pitch depends on amplitude or that louder sounds travel faster.","Thinking frequency changes when a wave enters a new medium (frequency stays the same; speed and wavelength change).","Putting the EM spectrum in the wrong order (mixing up microwaves/radio, or UV/X-rays). Use: Raging Martians Invaded Venus Using X-ray Guns.","Typing ⟪3.0\\times 10^{8}⟫ on the calculator as ⟪3\\times 10EXP8⟫ (gives ten times too much).","Giving vague uses ('communication', 'medicine') instead of specific ones ('mobile phones', 'killing cancer cells')."],"mcq":[{"id":"week07.check.m1","kind":"mcq","q":"A wave transfers","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["matter only","energy only, without transferring matter","both energy and matter","neither energy nor matter"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A wave carries energy; the particles of the medium only vibrate about fixed positions. C is wrong because the medium is not carried along (a cork just bobs).","section":"p01"},{"id":"week07.check.m2","kind":"mcq","q":"In a longitudinal wave, the particles of the medium vibrate","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["at right angles to the direction of travel","parallel to the direction of travel","in circles","only when the wave is in a vacuum"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Longitudinal = vibration along the direction of travel. A describes a transverse wave.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week07.check.m3","kind":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is a longitudinal wave?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["Light","Radio waves","Sound","X-rays"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Sound is longitudinal. Light, radio and X-rays are all EM waves, which are transverse.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week07.check.m4","kind":"mcq","q":"A wave has a frequency of 20 Hz. Its period is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["0.05 s","0.5 s","20 s","200 s"],"answer":"A","explanation":"⟪T=1/f=1/20=0.05\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫. D (and C) come from multiplying instead of dividing.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week07.check.m5","kind":"mcq","q":"A wave of frequency 200 Hz has a wavelength of 1.5 m. Its speed is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["0.0075 m s^−1^","133 m s^−1^","300 m s^−1^","201.5 m s^−1^"],"answer":"C","explanation":"⟪v=f\\lambda =200\\times 1.5=300\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫. B comes from dividing 200 by 1.5.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week07.check.m6","kind":"mcq","q":"The vertical distance from a crest to a trough of a water wave is 12 cm. The amplitude is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["3 cm","6 cm","12 cm","24 cm"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Amplitude is measured from the rest position to a crest = half of crest-to-trough ⟪=6\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫. C is the common mistake of using the full height.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week07.check.m7","kind":"mcq","q":"On a displacement–TIME graph of a wave, the horizontal distance for one complete cycle represents the","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["amplitude","wavelength","period","speed"],"answer":"C","explanation":"On a time axis, one cycle is the period. B (wavelength) is read from a displacement–POSITION graph.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week07.check.m8","kind":"mcq","q":"When a sound wave passes from air into water, which quantity stays the SAME?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["Speed","Wavelength","Frequency","Both speed and wavelength"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Frequency is fixed by the source. Speed increases in water, so wavelength increases too ⟪(\\lambda =v/f)⟫; A and B change.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week07.check.m9","kind":"mcq","q":"An electric bell rings inside a jar. As the air is pumped out, the sound becomes fainter. This shows that sound","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["is a transverse wave","needs a medium to travel","travels faster than light","cannot be reflected"],"answer":"B","explanation":"With less air there is less medium to carry the sound. A is wrong — sound is longitudinal, and the experiment does not test that.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week07.check.m10","kind":"mcq","q":"A note of higher PITCH has a higher","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["amplitude","speed","frequency","wavelength"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Pitch depends on frequency. A (amplitude) controls loudness, not pitch; speed is the same for all notes in air.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week07.check.m11","kind":"mcq","q":"A boy hears the echo of his shout from a cliff 1.0 s after shouting. If the speed of sound is 340 m s^−1^, the cliff is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["170 m away","340 m away","680 m away","0.34 m away"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Sound travels there and back: ⟪2d=vt=340\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫, so ⟪d=170\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫. B forgets to halve the distance.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week07.check.m12","kind":"mcq","q":"We can hear people talking around a corner because sound waves are","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["reflected","refracted","diffracted","polarised"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Diffraction is the spreading of waves round edges and through gaps. Reflection (A) can help in a room but the property that bends sound round a corner is diffraction.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week07.check.m13","kind":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is NOT a use of ultrasound?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["Scanning an unborn baby","Measuring the depth of the sea","Cleaning jewellery","Sterilising surgical instruments"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Instruments are sterilised with gamma rays (or UV), not ultrasound. A, B and C are standard uses of ultrasound (ultrasonic baths clean, but do not sterilise).","section":"p01"},{"id":"week07.check.m14","kind":"mcq","q":"Which list is in order of INCREASING frequency?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["Radio, infrared, ultraviolet, gamma","Gamma, X-rays, visible, radio","Microwaves, radio, visible, X-rays","Visible, infrared, microwaves, radio"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Frequency increases from radio → microwaves → IR → visible → UV → X-rays → gamma. B and D are in order of DECREASING frequency; C puts radio after microwaves.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week07.check.m15","kind":"mcq","q":"Which electromagnetic wave is used in TV remote controls?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["Ultraviolet","Infrared","Microwaves","X-rays"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Remote controls use infrared. Microwaves (C) are used for mobile phones and cooking.","section":"p01"}],"structured":[{"id":"week07.check.s1","kind":"multi","title":"Describing waves","total":13,"stem":["A student shakes one end of a long rope up and down steadily. The diagram shows the shape of the rope at one instant."],"stemBlocks":[{"type":"img","src":"img/week07/dp_numbers","alt":"Displacement–position graph of the rope","caption":"Displacement–position graph of the rope","width":4.6,"w":1174,"h":574}],"parts":[{"id":"week07.check.s1.a","kind":"self","q":"State whether the wave on the rope is transverse or longitudinal. Explain your answer.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Transverse (1); the rope (particles) moves up and down, at right angles to the direction the wave travels (along the rope) (1).","points":[{"text":"Transverse","marks":1},{"text":"the rope (particles) moves up and down, at right angles to the direction the wave travels (along the rope)","marks":1}],"label":"(a)"},{"id":"week07.check.s1.b","kind":"self","q":"Define the terms (i) amplitude and (ii) wavelength.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"(i) Maximum displacement from the rest position (1). (ii) Distance between successive points in phase, e.g. crest to crest (1).","points":[{"text":"(i) Maximum displacement from the rest position","marks":1},{"text":"(ii) Distance between successive points in phase, e.g. crest to crest","marks":1}],"label":"(b)"},{"id":"week07.check.s1.c","kind":"self","q":"Use the graph to determine (i) the amplitude and (ii) the wavelength of the wave.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"(i) 3.0 cm (1). (ii) 40 cm / 0.40 m (1).","points":[{"text":"(i) 3.0 cm","marks":1},{"text":"(ii) 40 cm / 0.40 m","marks":1}],"label":"(c)"},{"id":"week07.check.s1.d","kind":"self","q":"The student's hand makes 15 complete oscillations in 3.0 s. Calculate (i) the frequency, (ii) the period of the wave.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"(i) ⟪f=15/3.0⟫ (1) ⟪=5.0\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫ (1). (ii) ⟪T=1/f=0.20\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"(i) ⟪f=15/3.0⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=5.0\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"(ii) ⟪T=1/f=0.20\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫","marks":1}],"label":"(d)"},{"id":"week07.check.s1.e","kind":"self","q":"Calculate the speed of the wave along the rope.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪v=f\\lambda =5.0\\times 0.40⟫ (1) ⟪=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪v=f\\lambda =5.0\\times 0.40⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":2,"sf":2,"unit":"m s^−1^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}"},"label":"(e)","autoKind":"num"},{"id":"week07.check.s1.f","kind":"self","q":"The student now shakes the rope twice as often (same rope, same tension). State what happens to (i) the speed and (ii) the wavelength.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"(i) Speed stays the same — it depends on the rope/medium (1). (ii) Wavelength halves, to 0.20 m (1).","points":[{"text":"(i) Speed stays the same — it depends on the rope/medium","marks":1},{"text":"(ii) Wavelength halves, to 0.20 m","marks":1}],"label":"(f)"}]},{"id":"week07.check.s2","kind":"multi","title":"Sound and echoes","total":14,"stem":["A class investigates sound in the school field."],"stemBlocks":[],"parts":[{"id":"week07.check.s2.a","kind":"self","q":"Explain how a vibrating loudspeaker cone produces a sound wave in the air. Use the terms compression and rarefaction.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"Cone moves forward, pushing air molecules together → compression (1); cone moves back, molecules spread out → rarefaction (1); these travel outward as the molecules pass on the vibration along the direction of travel (longitudinal) (1).","points":[{"text":"Cone moves forward, pushing air molecules together → compression","marks":1},{"text":"cone moves back, molecules spread out → rarefaction","marks":1},{"text":"these travel outward as the molecules pass on the vibration along the direction of travel (longitudinal)","marks":1}],"label":"(a)"},{"id":"week07.check.s2.b","kind":"self","q":"A student stands 102 m from a tall wall and claps two blocks together. She hears the echo 0.60 s later. Calculate the speed of sound.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Distance ⟪=2\\times 102=204\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (1); ⟪v=204/0.60⟫ (1) ⟪=340\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"Distance ⟪=2\\times 102=204\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪v=204/0.60⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=340\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":340,"sf":2,"unit":"m s^−1^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}"},"label":"(b)","autoKind":"num"},{"id":"week07.check.s2.c","kind":"self","q":"Suggest why the student's result would be more accurate if she stood further from the wall and timed 20 echoes.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Longer time measured (1), so the reaction-time error is a smaller fraction/percentage of the measured time (1).","points":[{"text":"Longer time measured","marks":1},{"text":"so the reaction-time error is a smaller fraction/percentage of the measured time","marks":1}],"label":"(c)"},{"id":"week07.check.s2.d","kind":"self","q":"During a storm the class sees lightning and hears the thunder 4.0 s later. Using your answer to (b), calculate how far away the lightning struck.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪d=vt=340\\times 4.0⟫ (1) ⟪=1360\\,\\mathrm{m}\\approx 1.4\\,\\mathrm{km}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪d=vt=340\\times 4.0⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=1360\\,\\mathrm{m}\\approx 1.4\\,\\mathrm{km}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":1.4,"sf":2,"unit":"km","unitTex":"\\mathrm{km}"},"label":"(d)","autoKind":"num"},{"id":"week07.check.s2.e","kind":"self","q":"Two notes are displayed on a CRO with the same settings. Note X has a taller trace than note Y; note Y has more waves across the screen. Compare the loudness and pitch of X and Y.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"X is louder (larger amplitude) (1); Y has the higher pitch (higher frequency) (1).","points":[{"text":"X is louder (larger amplitude)","marks":1},{"text":"Y has the higher pitch (higher frequency)","marks":1}],"label":"(e)"},{"id":"week07.check.s2.f","kind":"self","q":"A bat emits ultrasound of frequency 50 kHz. Calculate the wavelength in air ⟪(v=340\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1})⟫ and explain why humans cannot hear it.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪\\lambda =340/50\\,000=6.8\\times 10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\ (0.0068\\,\\mathrm{m})⟫ (1); 50 kHz is above the upper limit of human hearing, 20 kHz (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪\\lambda =340/50\\,000=6.8\\times 10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\ (0.0068\\,\\mathrm{m})⟫","marks":1},{"text":"50 kHz is above the upper limit of human hearing, 20 kHz","marks":1}],"label":"(f)"}]},{"id":"week07.check.s3","kind":"multi","title":"The electromagnetic spectrum","total":13,"stem":["The electromagnetic spectrum is shown below with some members missing."],"stemBlocks":[{"type":"table","headers":["radio","P","infrared","visible","Q","X-rays","R"],"rows":[["","","","","","",""]]}],"parts":[{"id":"week07.check.s3.a","kind":"self","q":"Name the waves P, Q and R.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"P: microwaves (1); Q: ultraviolet (1); R: gamma rays (1).","points":[{"text":"P: microwaves","marks":1},{"text":"Q: ultraviolet","marks":1},{"text":"R: gamma rays","marks":1}],"label":"(a)"},{"id":"week07.check.s3.b","kind":"self","q":"State TWO properties shared by all members of the spectrum.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Any two: transverse; same speed ⟪3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ in vacuum; no medium needed; transfer energy; can be reflected/refracted/diffracted (1 each).","points":[{"text":"Any two: transverse; same speed ⟪3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ in vacuum; no medium needed; transfer energy; can be reflected/refracted/diffracted (1 each).","marks":2,"each":true}],"label":"(b)"},{"id":"week07.check.s3.c","kind":"self","q":"State which member shown has (i) the lowest frequency, (ii) the shortest wavelength.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"(i) Radio waves (1). (ii) R / gamma rays (1).","points":[{"text":"(i) Radio waves","marks":1},{"text":"(ii) R / gamma rays","marks":1}],"label":"(c)"},{"id":"week07.check.s3.d","kind":"self","q":"Give ONE source and ONE use of Q.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Source: the Sun / mercury-vapour lamp / welding arc (1). Use: sterilising water/equipment / detecting forged notes / vitamin D (1).","points":[{"text":"Source: the Sun / mercury-vapour lamp / welding arc","marks":1},{"text":"Use: sterilising water/equipment / detecting forged notes / vitamin D","marks":1}],"label":"(d)"},{"id":"week07.check.s3.e","kind":"self","q":"A mobile phone signal has a frequency of ⟪9.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫. Calculate its wavelength.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪\\lambda =c/f=3.0\\times 10^{8}/9.0\\times 10^{8}⟫ (1) ⟪=0.33\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪\\lambda =c/f=3.0\\times 10^{8}/9.0\\times 10^{8}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=0.33\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":0.33,"sf":2,"unit":"m","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}"},"label":"(e)","autoKind":"num"},{"id":"week07.check.s3.f","kind":"self","q":"Explain why doctors use X-rays to look at a broken bone but use ultrasound to check an unborn baby.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"X-rays pass through soft tissue but are absorbed by bone, so bone shows clearly (1); X-rays are ionising and could harm the developing baby, whereas ultrasound is non-ionising/safe (1).","points":[{"text":"X-rays pass through soft tissue but are absorbed by bone, so bone shows clearly","marks":1},{"text":"X-rays are ionising and could harm the developing baby, whereas ultrasound is non-ionising/safe","marks":1}],"label":"(f)"}]}],"n":53},{"id":"week07.past","kind":"past","title":"Past-paper practice","titleText":"Past-paper practice","check":true,"log":true,"questions":[{"id":"week07.past.q1","kind":"multi","title":"Data analysis (Paper 02 Question 1 style) — speed of sound from frequency and wavelength","total":22,"stem":["A student used a signal generator, a loudspeaker and two microphones connected to a CRO to measure the wavelength, λ, of sound waves of different frequencies, f, in air. Her results are shown in Table 1."],"stemBlocks":[{"type":"table","headers":["Frequency, f / Hz","200","250","320","400","500","640","800"],"rows":[["Wavelength, λ / m","1.69","1.37","1.05","0.86","0.67","0.54","0.42"],["⟪1/f/\\times 10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫","","","","","","",""]]},{"type":"p","text":"Table 1"}],"parts":[{"id":"week07.past.q1.a","kind":"self","q":"Complete the last row of Table 1 by calculating 1/f for each frequency, in units of 10^−3^ s (e.g. for 200 Hz, ⟪1/f=0.005\\,\\mathrm{s}=5.00\\times 10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫).","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":1,"scheme":"5.00, 4.00, 3.13 (3.125), 2.50, 2.00, 1.56, 1.25 (3 marks all correct; −1 per error).","points":[{"text":"5.00, 4.00, 3.13 (3.125), 2.50, 2.00, 1.56, 1.25","marks":3}],"label":"(a)"},{"id":"week07.past.q1.b","kind":"self","q":"On the grid provided, plot a graph of λ (y-axis) against 1/f (x-axis). Draw the best-fit line.","marks":7,"level":3,"img":{"src":"img/week07/graph_paper","alt":"On the grid provided, plot a graph of λ (y-axis) against 1/f (x-axis). Draw the best-fit line.","w":1174,"h":934},"lines":0,"scheme":"Axes labelled with quantities and units: wavelength, λ / m and ⟪1/f/\\times 10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{s}\\ (1+1)⟫; suitable linear scale using more than half the grid, e.g. 1 big square ⟪=1\\times 10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫ and 0.5 m (1); all 7 points correct within half a small square (2; −1 per error); straight best-fit line passing through/near the origin (1); title 'Graph of λ against 1/f' (1).","points":[{"text":"Axes labelled with quantities and units: wavelength, λ / m and ⟪1/f/\\times 10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫","marks":2},{"text":"suitable linear scale using more than half the grid, e.g. 1 big square ⟪=1\\times 10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫ and 0.5 m","marks":1},{"text":"all 7 points correct within half a small square","marks":2},{"text":"straight best-fit line passing through/near the origin","marks":1},{"text":"title 'Graph of λ against 1/f'","marks":1}],"label":"(b)","grid":true},{"id":"week07.past.q1.c","kind":"self","q":"Describe the relationship between λ and 1/f shown by your graph.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Straight line through the origin (1) → λ is directly proportional to 1/f (λ is inversely proportional to f) (1).","points":[{"text":"Straight line through the origin","marks":1},{"text":"→ λ is directly proportional to 1/f (λ is inversely proportional to f)","marks":1}],"label":"(c)"},{"id":"week07.past.q1.d","kind":"self","q":"Determine the gradient of your graph. Show clearly the triangle used.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":4,"scheme":"Large triangle drawn (1); correct readings from the line, e.g. ⟪\\Delta \\lambda =1.70\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫, ⟪\\Delta (1/f)=5.00\\times 10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫ (1); gradient ⟪=1.70/5.00\\times 10^{-3}\\approx 340⟫ (accept 330–350) (1); unit m s^−1^ (1).","points":[{"text":"Large triangle drawn","marks":1},{"text":"correct readings from the line, e.g. ⟪\\Delta \\lambda =1.70\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫, ⟪\\Delta (1/f)=5.00\\times 10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"gradient ⟪=1.70/5.00\\times 10^{-3}\\approx 340⟫ (accept 330–350)","marks":1},{"text":"unit m s^−1^","marks":1}],"label":"(d)"},{"id":"week07.past.q1.e","kind":"self","q":"Use the equation ⟪v=f\\lambda⟫ to show that the gradient of the graph is the speed of sound, and state the speed of sound obtained.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪v=f\\lambda \\rightarrow \\lambda =v\\times (1/f)⟫ (1); compare with ⟪y=mx⟫: gradient ⟪=v⟫ (1); speed of sound ≈ 340 m s^−1^ (value from (d)) (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪v=f\\lambda \\rightarrow \\lambda =v\\times (1/f)⟫","marks":1},{"text":"compare with ⟪y=mx⟫: gradient ⟪=v⟫","marks":1},{"text":"speed of sound ≈ 340 m s^−1^ (value from (d))","marks":1}],"label":"(e)"},{"id":"week07.past.q1.f","kind":"self","q":"Use your graph to find the wavelength of a sound of frequency 300 Hz. Show on the graph how you obtained the value.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪1/f=3.33\\times 10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫, dashed lines drawn on the graph (1); ⟪\\lambda \\approx 1.13\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (accept 1.10–1.16 m) (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪1/f=3.33\\times 10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫, dashed lines drawn on the graph","marks":1},{"text":"⟪\\lambda \\approx 1.13\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (accept 1.10–1.16 m)","marks":1}],"num":{"value":1.13,"sf":3,"unit":"m","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}","min":1.1,"max":1.16},"label":"(f)","autoKind":"num"},{"id":"week07.past.q1.g","kind":"self","q":"State ONE precaution the student should take to obtain reliable results.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"Any one: repeat each measurement and average; measure the distance between microphones carefully with a metre rule (avoid parallax); avoid reflecting surfaces/background noise; keep temperature constant (1).","points":[{"text":"Any one: repeat each measurement and average; measure the distance between microphones carefully with a metre rule (avoid parallax); avoid reflecting surfaces/background noise; keep temperature constant","marks":1}],"label":"(g)"}]},{"id":"week07.past.q2","kind":"multi","title":"Extended response (Paper 02 Section B style) — sound, ultrasound and EM waves","total":15,"stem":[],"stemBlocks":[],"parts":[{"id":"week07.past.q2.a","kind":"self","q":"Describe an experiment to show that sound needs a material medium to travel. Your answer should include a labelled diagram, the method and the expected observations.","marks":5,"level":3,"lines":10,"scheme":"Diagram: bell jar with electric bell inside, connected to vacuum pump, power supply outside (1). Method: switch on bell — heard and seen ringing (1); pump air out gradually (1). Observation: sound gets fainter until almost inaudible (1); hammer still seen striking — light passes through vacuum but sound does not → sound needs a medium (1).","points":[{"text":"Diagram: bell jar with electric bell inside, connected to vacuum pump, power supply outside","marks":1},{"text":"Method: switch on bell — heard and seen ringing","marks":1},{"text":"pump air out gradually","marks":1},{"text":"Observation: sound gets fainter until almost inaudible","marks":1},{"text":"hammer still seen striking — light passes through vacuum but sound does not → sound needs a medium","marks":1}],"label":"(a)"},{"id":"week07.past.q2.b","kind":"self","q":"Give TWO differences between sound waves and radio waves.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Sound longitudinal, radio transverse (1); sound needs a medium, radio can travel through a vacuum / speed: sound ≈ 340 m s^−1^, radio ⟪3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"Sound longitudinal, radio transverse","marks":1},{"text":"sound needs a medium, radio can travel through a vacuum / speed: sound ≈ 340 m s^−1^, radio ⟪3.0\\times 10^{8}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1}],"label":"(b)"},{"id":"week07.past.q2.c","kind":"self","q":"A fishing boat uses ultrasound to find a shoal of fish. A pulse is sent straight down and an echo from the shoal returns after 0.12 s; an echo from the sea bed returns after 0.50 s. The speed of sound in sea water is 1500 m s^−1^. (i) Calculate the depth of the shoal. (ii) Calculate the distance between the shoal and the sea bed.","marks":5,"level":3,"lines":6,"scheme":"(i) ⟪d=vt/2⟫ (1) ⟪=1500\\times 0.12/2=90\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (1). (ii) Sea bed depth ⟪=1500\\times 0.50/2⟫ (1) ⟪=375\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (1); distance ⟪=375-90=285\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"(i) ⟪d=vt/2⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=1500\\times 0.12/2=90\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"(ii) Sea bed depth ⟪=1500\\times 0.50/2⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=375\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"distance ⟪=375-90=285\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫","marks":1}],"label":"(c)"},{"id":"week07.past.q2.d","kind":"self","q":"State why ultrasound, rather than audible sound, is used in (c), and give ONE other use of ultrasound.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"Any two reasons: short wavelength gives more detailed echoes / narrow beam that spreads less / cannot be heard so does not disturb crew (1)(1); other use: prenatal scanning / cleaning jewellery / crack detection in metals (1).","points":[{"text":"Any two reasons: short wavelength gives more detailed echoes / narrow beam that spreads less / cannot be heard so does not disturb crew","marks":2},{"text":"other use: prenatal scanning / cleaning jewellery / crack detection in metals","marks":1}],"label":"(d)"}]}],"n":54}]};
